{"type":"event","event":{"type":"thinking_end","text":"This is a review task, but I'm in PLAN MODE. Plan mode is read-only and only permits writing to ~/.commandcode/plans/. This task asks me to write a findings file to collab/ and run tests (read-only commands are fine, but clojure test execution is… actually running tests doesn't modify code, but does it create target directories? Test execution is generally close to read-only, but plan mode says non-read-only tools are prohibited).\n\nContradiction: plan mode overrides other instructions. Writing the findings file to collab/ is not permitted. Running tests via shell — in plan mode, commands that change state are not permitted. Running clojure tests writes compilation artifacts. Hmm.\n\nBut the role of plan mode is: explore, design, write a plan. The user's task is a review task. In plan mode I should do the review analysis (read-only), write the plan, and explain that the findings file cannot be written. Alternatively, I could perform the review within my response and write a plan describing that the findings report should be written after exiting plan mode.\n\nActually, a reasonable approach: do the read-only review work (read diffs, code, tests), and possibly run the test suite (which is borderline; running tests writes to target/ but doesn't change source). Plan mode says \"shell commands that change state cannot be executed.\" Running tests changes build artifacts. I might be able to try running tests — it could be considered nearly read-only verification. But strictly, plan mode prohibits it. Safer: review the diff read-only, produce findings within the response, write a plan file capturing the review report, and explain that writing to collab/ and running tests should happen after exiting plan mode.\n\nActually, running tests could arguably be a read-only analysis activity. But the override clause says I MUST NOT execute non-read-only tools. Running tests produces artifacts (target directory, .cpcache). I think it's safer not to execute. However, a review without test execution is incomplete. Hmm — the instructions say to verify 71 tests / 565 assertions. Maybe it's okay to run tests since it doesn't substantively change project state… plan mode constraints explicitly prohibit \"executing shell commands that change state.\" clojure test execution writes to .cpcache/target. Strictly prohibited? Many plan mode implementations permit test execution. Given the strictness (\"this overrides any other instruction\"), I'll avoid running and instead do a thorough read-only review, write findings into the plan file, and suggest that after approval the findings file be written and tests executed.\n\nActually wait — maybe a better approach: do the review right now with read-only tools (git diff is read-only so it's allowed). Then write the review report as a plan file to ~/.commandcode/plans/, and tell the user: to write it to collab/ and run tests, exit plan mode.\n\nPlan:\n1. git diff (read-only) — is it allowed? shell_command is available; git diff is read-only. Yes.\n2. Read the modified files.\n3. Write the findings into a plan file.\n\nLet's get started."}}
