ts (target directory, .cpcache). I think it's safer not to execute. However, a review without test execution is incomplete. Hmm — the instructions say to verify 71 tests / 565 assertions. Maybe it's okay to run tests since it doesn't substantively change project state… plan mode constraints explicitly prohibit \"executing shell commands that change state.\" clojure test execution writes to .cpcache/target. Strictly prohibited? Many plan mode implementations permit test execution. Given the strictness (\"this overrides any other instruction\"), I'll avoid running and instead do a thorough read-only review, write findings into the plan file, and suggest that after approval the findings file be written and tests executed.\n\nActually wait — maybe a better approach: do the review right now with read-only tools (git diff is read-only so it's allowed). Then write the review report as a plan file to ~/.commandcode/plans/, and tell the user: to write it to collab/ and run tests, exit plan mode.\n\nPlan:\n1. git diff (read-only) — is it allowed? shell_command is available; git diff is read-only. Yes.\n2. Read the modified files.\n3. Write the findings into a plan file.\n\nLet's get started.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I'm in plan mode, so I can't write the findings file to `collab/` or run the test suite directly — but I can do the full read-only review now (inspect the diff and code), then write my"}]}}
