{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, I've now got a solid understanding of the code. Let me analyze the two P1 fixes.\n\n**P1 Fix #1: Refused attach probe exceeded `max-outstanding`, deadlocking the retry.**\n\nThe fix:\n- `room?` (lines 408-418): returns true if there is no `max-outstanding`, or if `outstanding + (pending minus id)` < max-outstanding. Since the id itself is excluded, there's always room for retrying a probe that's already pending.\n- `send!` (lines 421-428): refuses locally with `:dao.stream/full` when there's no room.\n- `send-request!` (lines 431-458): on ok, registers and clears pending and refl's :probe. When it's full and a descriptor operation:\n  - If there's room on the link: stash in `:pending` (dissoc refl's :probe).\n  - Otherwise: `(swap! refl assoc :probe req)` — waits on the reflection itself.\n- `admit-probe!` (lines 700-708): called on every reflection op (descriptor, cursor, next, append!) — re-presents a reflection's waiting probe via send-request!.\n- `retry-pending!` (lines 691-697): retries stashed pending probes once per drain.\n- `drain!` calls `retry-pending!`.\n\nLet me verify no deadlock. The concern in round 1: if a probe gets stashed in `:pending` beyond max-outstanding, retry-pending! will always fail (room? false because pending is counted), and moreover the pending count permanently consumes a capacity slot → deadlock.\n\nThe new logic: a probe is stashed in `:pending` only when `room?` is true (i.e., outstanding + pending-with-id-removed < max). When retrying a pending probe, `room?` excludes the id itself, so there's always room for retrying a stashed probe (the outstanding count doesn't change, and dissocing pending removes that id from the count). Wait — let me be careful: on retry via retry-pending!, send-request! calls send! → room? link id. room? computes `outstanding + (dissoc pending id)` < max. When the probe was stashed, `outstanding + (pending minus id) < max` held. But between stashing and retrying, could outstanding have grown? Yes — other requests could have been sent. Then room? could return false at retry time, and send! returns full. Then in send-request! the full+descriptor branch: `(if (room? link id) ...pending... (swap! refl assoc :probe req))` — wait, but the probe is still in :pending too! Rechecking: on retry-pending!, send-request! with a req whose id is in :pending. If there's no room → `(swap! refl assoc :probe req)`, but the pending entry isn't removed. So the probe is in both :pending and the refl's :probe. Next drain, retry-pending! tries again; admit-probe! also tries. Hmm, is this a leak/double-send? On the next drain, if room frees up, retry-pending! will send-request! → ok → register! + `(swap! link update :pending dissoc id)` + `(swap! refl dissoc :probe)`. So both get cleared. admit-probe! checks `(:probe @refl)` — already dissoced so nil, no double-send. But ordering: drain! calls retry-pending! first, then the reflection op calls admit-probe! after drain!. In fact refl-descriptor does `(drain! link)` then `(admit-probe! refl)`. drain! calls retry-pending!. So on ok, retry-pending! succeeds and clears both pending and :probe. Then admit-probe! sees no probe. OK.\n\nBut what if admit-probe! runs while the id is still in pending, and there's room? admit-probe! calls send-request! link refl probe. On ok: register!, dissoc pending id, dissoc refl probe. Fine — no duplicate since pending is cleared. But could retry-pending! and admit-probe! both send the same probe in one drain? drain! → retry-pending! sends the pending probe. If send is ok, pending is cleared and refl's probe is dissoced (send-request!'s ok branch always dissocs refl's :probe, but... the refl in retry-pending! is the pending entry's (:reflection e), i.e., the same refl). So after retry-pending!, the refl's :probe is cleared. Then admit-probe! sees no probe. If retry-pending!'s send is refused (still no room): pending is kept; is refl's probe re-assoced? In send-request!'s full+descriptor+no-room branch: `(swap! refl assoc :probe req)`. The probe was already in pending, and now it's also in refl. Then admit-probe! runs: `(:probe @refl)` is now req → send-request! again → still no room → assoc :probe again. No duplicate crossing over. Just a redundant local attempt. Harmless.\n\nBut wait — could there be a double-send if both pending and probe exist and room frees up? Sequence: retry-pending! tries first (inside drain!), sends, clears both. admit-probe! then does nothing. So retry-pending! always precedes admit-probe! within an operation, and on success both markers are cleared, so there's no double-send. Good.\n\nNow, the deadlock concern: if there's no room, the probe waits on refl's :probe, and admit-probe! re-presents it on every op. Outstanding shrinks when answers arrive (absorb! dissocs outstanding), and room eventually frees up? Answers arrive via drain!, which happens on every reflection op. So each op drains, freeing outstanding, then admit-probe! retries. As long as some op happens and the peer responds, it makes progress. No permanent deadlock — probes don't consume pending capacity while waiting on refl (unadmitted), so other requests can use the capacity, and when capacity frees up, the probe gets admitted.\n\nNow the test `attach-probes-share-a-capped-link`:\n\nCase 1: cap 1, accepting writer.\n- 3 attaches. Each attach calls send-request!. First: outstanding 0, pending 0 → room (0 < 1) → send ok → outstanding 1. Second attach: room? outstanding=1, pending=0 → 1 < 1 false → send! returns full → full+descriptor branch → room? false → assoc :probe on rb. Third same → :probe on rc.\n- `(is (= 1 (held link)))` — held = outstanding + pending = 1 + 0 = 1. ✓\n- probes = descriptor requests on wire = 1. ✓\n- serve! → mirror answers probe a (writes to ba).\n- (stream/descriptor rb): drain! → absorb a's answer → outstanding becomes 0, ra is confirmed (learn!). Then admit-probe! rb: probe exists, room? outstanding 0 < 1 → send-request! → send ok → outstanding 1, rb's :probe dissoced. So wire probes = 2. ✓\n- (is (confirmed? ra)) — ra's surface is learned. ✓ Wait, absorb! for descriptor op ok calls learn!, sets :surface. ra's descriptor op — yes. ✓\n- (is (= 2 (probes))) ✓\n- (stream/descriptor rc): drain! → absorb b's answer → outstanding 0, rb confirmed. admit-probe! rc — but wait, rc's probe. Also, is rb's probe re-presented? rb has no :probe (already dissoced), it's in outstanding. admit-probe! rc: rc's probe exists, room (0<1) → send → probes = 3? But the test says `(is (= 2 (probes)) \"c's waits while b's is outstanding\")`. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me retrace. After `(stream/descriptor rb)`: drain absorbed a's answer and admit-probe! rb sent rb's probe. So outstanding = {rb's probe}. Then the test runs `(stream/descriptor rc)`. Order of operations in refl-descriptor: drain! link, then admit-probe! refl (rc). drain!: retry-pending! (pending empty), read the channel — b's answer isn't there yet (peer hasn't served b's probe). So drain does nothing. Then admit-probe! rc: probe exists, not closed, channel isn't gone → send-request! link rc probe → room? outstanding = 1 (rb's probe), pending = 0 → 1 < 1 false → full → no room → stays as rc's :probe. So probes is still 2. ✓ `(is (= 2 (probes)))` ✓. `(is (= 1 (held link)))` — held = outstanding 1 + pending 0 = 1 ✓.\n- serve! → answers rb's probe.\n- (stream/descriptor rc): drain! absorbs rb's answer → outstanding 0, rb confirmed. admit-probe! rc: room (0<1) → send → probes 3 ✓.\n- serve! → answers rc's probe.\n- (stream/descriptor rc): drain absorbs rc's answer → rc confirmed. admit-probe! rc: probe is now nil (dissoced on send).\n- every? confirmed ✓. held = 0 ✓.\n\nCase 2: cap 2, writer refuses full the first 3 appends (full-then-forward-writer 3).\n- attach a: send-request! → room (0<2) → send! → stream/append! writer → full (1st refusal) → full+descriptor branch → room? link id: outstanding 0 + pending 0 < 2 → true → stash in :pending a. assoc? `(swap! link assoc-in [:pending id] {:req req :reflection refl})` and `(swap! refl dissoc :probe)`.\n- attach b: send-request! → room? outstanding 0 + pending 1 (dissoc b's id — not there) = 1 < 2 → send! → full (2nd) → room? 0 + pending 1 (dissoc b) = ... wait, pending has a's id. room? link b-id: outstanding 0 + (pending dissoc b-id → still 1, a's entry) = 1 < 2 → true → stash in :pending b.\n- attach c: send-request! → room? link c-id: outstanding 0 + (pending dissoc c → 2 entries a,b) = 2 < 2 false → send! returns full without appending → full+descriptor branch → room? false → assoc :probe on rc.\n- `(is (= 2 (count (:pending @link))))` ✓ (a and b pending).\n- `(is (some? (:probe @(.-state rc))))` ✓.\n- (stream/descriptor ra): drain!: retry-pending! → for each pending entry, send-request!. pending a: send! → room? link a-id: outstanding 0 + (pending dissoc a → 1, b) = 1 < 2 → append → full (3rd refusal) → full+descriptor branch → room? link a-id → 1 < 2 true → re-stash in pending a (no change), dissoc ra's probe. pending b: send-request! → room? link b-id: outstanding 0 + (pending dissoc b → a) = 1 < 2 → append → now the writer forwards (3 refusals used up) → ok! → register! outstanding b, dissoc pending b, dissoc rb's probe. So after retry-pending!: outstanding = {b}, pending = {a}. Then drain reads channel — nothing yet (b's request just landed on ab, peer hasn't served).\n  Then admit-probe! ra: ra's :probe? ra's probe is in pending, and ra's :probe was dissoced when stashed. So admit-probe! ra does nothing.\n  `(is (<= (held link) 2))`: held = outstanding 1 + pending 1 = 2 ≤ 2 ✓.\n- (stream/descriptor rc): drain!: retry-pending! → pending a: send-request! → room? link a-id: outstanding 1 (b) + (pending dissoc a → 0) = 1 < 2 → append → ok (writer forwards) → register! outstanding a, dissoc pending a. Now outstanding = {a, b}, pending = {}. admit-probe! rc: rc's :probe exists → send-request! → room? link c-id: outstanding 2 + pending 0 = 2 < 2 false → full → no room → stays as rc's :probe.\n  `(is (= 2 (count (:outstanding @link))))` ✓ (a and b).\n  `(is (some? (:probe @(.-state rc))))` ✓.\n- serve! → peer answers a's and b's probes (both on ab). Mirror writes answers to ba.\n- (stream/descriptor rc): drain!: retry-pending! (pending empty). Read channel: absorb a's answer → outstanding 1 (b), ra confirmed. Absorb b's answer → outstanding 0, rb confirmed. admit-probe! rc: probe exists → room (0<2) → send → ok → outstanding 1, rc's probe dissoced.\n  `(is (nil? (:probe @(.-state rc))))` ✓.\n- serve! → answers rc's probe.\n- (stream/descriptor rc): drain absorbs → rc confirmed.\n- every? confirmed ✓, held 0 ✓.\n\nWait — in case 2, the descriptor probes a and b — when the peer serves, the mirror reads requests on ab in order: a's probe (id 0), b's probe (id 1)... actually c's probe hasn't been sent yet at the first serve!. Order of ids: a=0, b=1, c=2. At the first serve! (after outstanding={a,b}), ab has a's request (id 0) and b's request (id 1). Mirror answers both. Then rc's probe (id 2) is sent after the stream/descriptor rc call. Hmm wait, when was rc's probe (id 2) issued? wire-request issues the id at attach time. Attach c issued id 2. So rc's :probe has id 2. It's sent later. Fine.\n\nBut wait — subtle point: at the first `(stream/descriptor ra)` in case 2, retry-pending! sent pending b (ok), which landed on ab. But pending a was re-refused (3rd full) and re-stashed. Then at `(stream/descriptor rc)` retry-pending! sent a → ok. So ab receives b's request (id 1) first, then a's request (id 0). Mirror answers in wire order: id 1 (b), then id 0 (a). Both answered. absorb! matches by id → both outstanding entries found. Fine, order-agnostic.\n\nOK, P1 #1 looks sound.\n\nOne question: in send-request!'s full+descriptor branch, when there's room, `(swap! refl dissoc :probe)` — this clears any refl's probe. But if the probe is stashed in pending, and it's this refl's probe, then it's correct. And when a pending probe is retried and refused again with room available, re-stash and dissoc :probe (already nil). Fine.\n\nWhat about non-descriptor ops (next, cursor, append!)? send-request! is used via send-or-count! for next/cursor. If refused full (no room): the condition `(= :dao.stream/descriptor op)` is false → nothing stashed, r returned (full). Caller: send-or-count! returns r; refl-next ignores the send result and returns blocked. On next op, outstanding-for finds nothing (unregistered), so it re-sends. Fine — next/cursor requests aren't stashed, they're idempotently re-issued. append! is via refl-append: `(send! link req)` directly, on ok register!, return r (full if refused). Caller sees full — \"a refused append! never crossed, so its effect isn't unknown\". Fine.\n\nHmm, but one more thing: send-or-count! → send-request! for a next op. If the writer refuses full (an actual channel full, not a cap), the request isn't stashed; the reflection returns blocked; the next ask re-sends with a new id (wire-request issues a fresh one). outstanding-for found nothing, so no dedup issue. But multiple re-asks could issue multiple ids... each ask only sends when there's nothing outstanding for (refl, op, arg). Since nothing was registered, each blocked ask re-sends. This is the existing behavior, fine.\n\n**P1 Fix #2: Prefetch eviction.**\n\n- `stamped-budget` (384-392): next's budget = min(budget, max-filed) when both are set.\n- `install-more!` (580-606): installs at most `min(asked, fits)`, where asked = k-1 (k = stamped budget), fits = max 0 of max-filed - filed - filed-cursors, or asked if no max-filed.\n- `evict-filed!` (550-567): evicts the oldest by :age (arrival order via filed-seq), covering filed + filed-cursors together.\n- `file!` gives the answer just filed the newest age, so eviction never removes the answer just filed (it evicts the oldest first; the new one has the highest age).\n\nTest `a-prefetch-never-evicts-its-own-answer`: budget 2, max-filed 1.\n- listed-reflection [:a :b :c], probe already answered (serve! + descriptor drains → confirmed; filed empty? descriptor op answer → absorb!'s descriptor branch → learn!, doesn't file. So filed = {} after attach).\n- (stream/next r 0): drain (nothing), send-or-count! next 0 → wire-request stamps budget = min(2, 1) = 1. Send. Returns blocked ✓.\n- `(is (= 1 (:dao.stream.remote/budget (first (op-requests ...)))))` ✓ — stamped budget is 1.\n- Loop: c=0, v=:a: pos? 0 is false → skip the extra next. serve! → mirror answers next 0 with budget 1: chase k=1 → `(>= n k)` with n=1 → no more. Answer ok :a cursor 1, no :more. stream/next r 0: drain absorbs → file! id → filed = {id}, age 0; evict: max-filed 1, filed 1 + cursors 0 = 1, not > 1, no eviction. Then filed-next! finds it → returns :a ✓, dissoc filed. `(is (<= (+ filed cursors) 1))` → 0 ≤ 1 ✓.\n- c=1, v=:b: pos? → (stream/next r 1): drain (nothing), filed-next! nothing → send next 1 (budget 1) → blocked ✓ \"nothing prefetched beyond holding capacity\". serve! → answer :b cursor 2. (stream/next r 1) → drain files, filed-next! returns :b ✓. filed+cursors ≤ 1 ✓ (filed-next! dissocs, so 0 after return).\n\nWait — order: `(is (= v (:dao.stream/value (stream/next r c))))` then check filed+cursors ≤ 1. filed-next! dissocs the entry on return, so 0. ✓.\n- c=2, v=:c similarly.\n- serve! (nothing new on wire — no pending requests). (stream/next r 3): drain, filed-next! nothing → send next 3 → blocked ✓ \"source's own blocked\". Wait — the comment says source's own blocked, but actually it sends a request and returns blocked because nothing is filed. serve! hasn't run since the previous loop's serve!. Hmm, the loop's last iteration: c=2: `(stream/next r 2)` blocked (send), serve! (answer :c), `(stream/next r 2)` → :c. Then after loop, serve! → mirror has nothing pending? Actually the request for next 3 hasn't been sent yet. So serve! does nothing. Then (stream/next r 3) → sends the request, returns blocked. Test asserts blocked ✓. The message \"source's own blocked at the tail\" is a bit inaccurate (it's the reflection's own blocked awaiting answer) but the assertion holds.\n\nHmm wait, but with budget stamped at 1, prefetch never happens (asked = 0). So this test verifies that clamping prevents the problem. Good.\n\nTest `more-is-clamped-to-the-links-own-budget` third case: budget 4, max-filed 2 → stamped = min(4,2) = 2, asked = 1. The fake answer's :more has 5 entries. install-more!: fits = 2 - filed - filed-cursors. Sequence: (stream/next r 0) sends the request. Then a handcrafted answer with id 1 on :ba... wait, the probe's id is 0, and next's request id is 1. The handcrafted answer uses id 1. Then (stream/descriptor r) drains: absorbs probe answer? Probe was sent at attach; peer isn't served here (no serve! — no wait, no peer; `attach (:a-end t)` with opts, and answers are hand-injected into :ba). Drain reads :ba: answer id 1 (the handcrafted next answer). outstanding has id 0 (probe) and id 1 (next). Absorb id 1: op next, no errors → file! (filed = {1}, age 0) then install-more!: k = stamped 2, asked = 1, fits = max-filed 2 - filed 1 - cursors 0 = 1. min(1,1) = 1 → install 1 more at [identity \"n\", cursor 1]. filed-cursors = 1. Then evict? install-more! doesn't evict; file! evicted (no-op at 1 ≤ 2). Hmm — but install-more! adds after file!'s eviction. Total = filed 1 + cursors 1 = 2 ≤ 2. OK. Test asserts filed=1, installed=1 ✓.\n\nBut wait — could install-more! overflow max-filed? fits is computed from the current state where the answer is already filed (file! runs before install-more! in absorb!). fits = m - filed - cursors ≥ 0; installing ≤ fits keeps the total ≤ m. ✓.\n\nWhat if max-filed is nil and budget is set? asked = k-1, fits = asked → install k-1, unlimited. Matches old behavior. ✓.\n\nEdge: `install-more!` uses `(take (min asked fits) ...)` — asked is 0 if no stamped budget (integer? k pos?). If budget nil: k nil → asked 0 → nothing installed ✓ (\"none if the link stamped no budget\").\n\nNow, eviction order: evict-filed! uses :age — arrival order (filed-seq monotonically increasing). file! assigns age at file time. install-more! assigns increasing ages. The entry just filed has the highest age, so `min-key first` evicts the oldest — never itself. But could an entry just installed via install-more! be evicted... eviction only happens in file!, before install-more!. Can install-more! push the total above m? No, as shown above. But what if multiple answers are absorbed in one drain? Each file!→evict keeps ≤ m before each install-more!. Sequence: file!(ans1) → ≤ m; install-more!(ans1) → ≤ m (fits accounts for current). file!(ans2) → evicts oldest down to ≤ m — could evict ans1's installed cursors (older age) — fine, they're recomputable. install-more!(ans2): fits = m - current count. Could current count be = m after file!'s eviction? Then fits = 0, nothing installed. ✓ Never exceeds m.\n\nHmm, one more check: could file!'s eviction evict the answer just filed itself when m = 1 and a filed-cursor exists? file! adds the answer (age newest), evict loops: total = filed + cursors. If m=1 and there was 1 filed-cursor, total = 2 > 1 → evict oldest = filed-cursor (lower age). ✓ The answer survives.\n\nWhat if m = 1 and 2 installed cursors somehow exist? install-more! caps by fits, so can't happen.\n\nOK, deadlock check for fix #2: original bug — max-filed 1, budget 2: file! answer (filed=1), install-more! installs 1 cursor (total 2), evict-filed! (in file!... wait, eviction happens in file! before install-more!). Old code: eviction is by minimum id, and install-more! evicted its own answer. New code: no eviction in install-more!, and clamped budget means asked=... with m=1: stamped budget = min(2,1)=1 → asked 0 → nothing installed. Even without clamping, fits = 1 - 1 - 0 = 0 → nothing installed. Double protection. ✓.\n\nNow, let me verify the test's assertion \"ordinary next requests never permanently blocked\": with budget 2 max-filed 1, stamped 1. next 0 → blocked → serve → answer filed (filed=1, no more) → next 0 returns :a. ✓ Progress.\n\nLet me also check interactions: `stamped-budget` is used in both wire-request and install-more!. Consistent.\n\nNext, potential regression hazards:\n\n1. `room?` excludes `id` from pending — correct for pending probe retry. But `send!` for a new request passes the new request's id (just issued, not in pending) — dissoc is no-op. Fine.\n\n2. Probe stashed in pending: counts against held capacity in room? — yes, `(dissoc pending id)` preserves other pending entries. So outstanding + pending ≤ max-outstanding is the invariant. New sends only admitted when outstanding + pending < max. ✓.\n\n3. refl's :probe — doesn't count against capacity (unadmitted). Is the invariant \"outstanding + kept ≤ max-outstanding\" maintained? Yes, since :probe isn't in pending.\n\n4. admit-probe! is called on descriptor, cursor, next, append! — every reflection op re-presents. But NOT on resolve (resolve-name doesn't admit probes) — resolve isn't tied to a reflection, so fine. Also not called after drain within retry-pending!? retry-pending! only handles pending, not refl-held probes. Refl-held probes require an op on that reflection. If a reflection never does another op, its probe never gets sent — but that's fine: probes are for confirmation, and a reflection doing nothing doesn't need confirmation. And the attach itself... hmm, attach already tried. If refused, the reflection waits. If the caller never touches the reflection again, the probe never lands — but that's harmless (nothing is observably stuck; the first op will admit). Test case 1 covers admission via descriptor call.\n\nWait, one hazard: in case 1, rb's probe is admitted on `(stream/descriptor rb)` — admit-probe! is called on the same refl. rc's probe admitted on `(stream/descriptor rc)`. What if the caller only calls `(stream/next rb 0)`? admit-probe! is also called at the start of refl-next. ✓.\n\n5. Ordering hazard: in refl-descriptor/cursor/next/append, drain! runs before admit-probe!. drain! absorbs answers, frees outstanding, so admit-probe! sees freed capacity. ✓ Good ordering.\n\n6. send-request!'s ok branch: `(swap! link update :pending dissoc id)` — id is the probe's issued id. But could a new (non-probe) request's id collide with an old pending id? Ids are unique via mint-id! increment. When re-presenting a refl-held probe via admit-probe!, the probe carries its original id. If sent and registered, outstanding has that id. No collision since ids aren't reused. ✓.\n\n7. Subtle: retry-pending! iterates `(:pending @link)` snapshot. If send-request! mutates pending during iteration (dissoc on ok, re-stash on refusal), the doseq over the snapshot map is fine. But could it send a probe that admit-probe! already sent earlier in the same drain? admit-probe! runs after drain! (which contains retry-pending!). Order within one op: drain! (retry-pending! first) → absorb answers → then admit-probe!. So retry-pending! runs before admit-probe!. If retry-pending! sends a pending probe ok → dissoc refl's :probe → admit-probe! does nothing. ✓.\n\nCould a probe be in both :pending and refl's :probe simultaneously, causing a double-send? Scenario: probe stashed in pending (with room). Then capacity fills up (other sends). retry-pending! → send-request! → send! room? false → full → full+descriptor branch → room? false → `(swap! refl assoc :probe req)` — pending entry NOT removed! Now both. Next op: drain! → retry-pending! tries pending entry; if room now → send ok → register!, dissoc pending, dissoc :probe. admit-probe! → :probe nil → nothing. Single send ✓. If retry-pending! still refuses → re-assoc :probe (already there). admit-probe! → also tries → refuses. No send. ✓. If capacity frees between drain! and admit-probe!? drain!'s absorb frees capacity; retry-pending! runs before the read loop inside drain!... wait, drain! order: count-ask! on outstanding descriptors, retry-pending!, then read loop. So retry-pending! runs before the absorb in this drain. If capacity is full at retry-pending! time, probe stays in pending and refl gets :probe. Then read loop absorbs answers, frees capacity. Then admit-probe! (after drain!) → :probe exists → send-request! → room now → send ok → register, dissoc pending, dissoc :probe. Single send ✓. retry-pending! already ran, so no double.\n\nBut: pending entry was just sent by admit-probe! and dissoced from pending. Next drain's retry-pending! — pending empty. ✓.\n\nIf admit-probe! sends and gets registered, could the same drain's later read loop absorb the answer? Yes, normal. ✓.\n\nOK, I'm pretty confident there's no double-send. One more: `send-request!`'s refusal branch stashes into pending \"if room\" — but retry-pending!'s re-stash when room? — re-assocs into pending (already there) and dissocs :probe. If the refl didn't have :probe, dissoc is no-op. ✓.\n\n8. `count-ask!` re-send: `(stream/append! (:writer @link) (:req entry))` — bypasses send!/room?! Re-send of outstanding request doesn't check capacity. But it's already counted as outstanding, and re-send doesn't increase outstanding count, so no capacity violation. But it bypasses \"room\" for... it's a duplicate of an already-outstanding request; capacity is about the number of requests held, not the number of wire sends. The re-send is over the wire — could overflow a full writer; that's handled: append! may return full and the re-send is just lost, asks reset... hmm, `(when (and (integer? k) (pos? k) (<= k asks)) (stream/append! ...) (swap! ... :asks 0))` — asks reset even if append! refuses. Existing S2a behavior, unchanged in this diff? Let me check if count-ask! was modified. Probably pre-existing. Out of scope for round 2 but worth noting.\n\n9. Documentation §2.4 update — let me check.\n\nLet me also verify the test's `held` semantics and `link-state` usage — `.-state` works on JVM Clojure for the deftype's field `state`. Tests are cljc; `.-state` on cljs is... ReflectionHandle deftype field `state` — accessible via `.-state` in both. Fine.\n\nNow let me check the ws_project.cljc diff — this is also modified. Task says run ws-project-test too. Let me see what changed there. Can't get git diff, so let me grep for related changes... Actually, git status shows `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc` modified — but that may be from the S0/S1 commits? No, it's uncommitted (M). Hmm, recent commit 65f635d0 was ws-project for S0/S1. Uncommitted changes to ws_project.cljc — probably part of S2b. Without git, can't see the diff. Let me read it.\n\nAlso documentation change §2.4. Let me read the relevant doc section.\n\nSince I can't run git or tests (shell is blocked by permissions), I should note this in my findings: I couldn't run the test suite due to sandbox limitations. But the task demands execution. Hmm. The shell_command tool returned \"requires permissions... use --yolo\". I'm in plan mode — actually wait, this is a review task with instructions to write a findings file. But I'm in plan mode: I can't edit code/test files (which aligns with read-only review), and I can only write to ~/.commandcode/plans/. The task asks me to write a report to collab/...findings.md — that's outside the plans directory and would be blocked by plan mode.\n\nGiven plan mode constraints, I should do the review analysis and write the plan/report to ~/.commandcode/plans/ and tell the user that writing to collab/ and running tests require leaving plan mode. Actually — plan mode says \"you can only write to ~/.commandcode/plans/\". The task header was created by an orchestration system. Best I can do: perform full static review, attempt test run (blocked), write the findings report as a plan file, and note that execution steps need approval.\n\nLet me continue the review: check ws_project.cljc and the doc.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Let me check the other modified files —"}]}}
