{"type":"event","event":{"type":"tool_completed","toolCallId":"call_0ec6f35c988d45d5b3273d47","toolName":"read_file","result":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 70 lines\nNote: showing lines 260-329. More of the file remains. Use offset=330 to continue.\n260: The mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or an\n261: outcome. The served stream is the original: its cursors, its positions, its\n262: `gap`, its `end`. This is the whole basis of the claim in the title, and it\n263: rests on cursors being plain data that survive the codec (OD-3 decision 2,\n264: accepted). A handle whose cursors hold a host object cannot be served, and\n265: a composition refuses to enter it into a table.\n266: \n267: ### 2.4 The reflection\n268: \n269: `attach!` on a remote descriptor returns `:dao.stream/ok` at once with a\n270: reflection handle and a local, opaque `:dao.stream/attachment`. This is the\n271: contract's deferred remote confirmation (`dao.stream.md`, attach outcomes).\n272: The host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\n273: shared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\n274: writer; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\n275: outstanding request ids with the request each was sent for; filed answers\n276: keyed by id; the `more` outcomes installed at the cursor that precedes\n277: each; an optional event writer; and the policy data\n278: `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`, `:dao.stream.remote/budget` and the\n279: three bounds below, each a positive integer or nil for unbounded, any other\n280: value a composition error at `links`\n281: (`ex-info \"invalid DaoStream remote link policy\" {:policy policy}`):\n282: \n283: - `:dao.stream.remote/drain-budget`: the most channel reads one drain takes.\n284: - `:dao.stream.remote/max-outstanding`: the most requests the link holds\n285:   outstanding or kept unsent. A send that would pass it is refused locally\n286:   as though the writer answered `full`, nothing crossing: `cursor` and\n287:   `resolve` answer `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true`, `next`\n288:   answers `blocked`, and `append!` answers `{:dao.stream/outcome\n289:   :dao.stream/full}`, its effect known. A refused attach probe is kept on\n290:   the link only while there is room under the same bound, so outstanding\n291:   plus kept never exceeds it and each kept probe's retry, which does not\n292:   count itself, always has room. A probe without room waits on its own\n293:   reflection, and that reflection's next operation offers it again.\n294: - `:dao.stream.remote/max-filed`: the most filed answers plus installed\n295:   `more` outcomes the link retains. Past it the oldest retained is evicted,\n296:   in the order the link retained them, so filing an answer never evicts\n297:   that answer. A prefetch only fills free capacity and never evicts: no\n298:   more outcomes are installed than fit beside what is retained, and a\n299:   `next` request's stamped budget is at most `max-filed`.\n300:   Everything filed is idempotent-recomputable and `append!` answers are\n301:   never filed, so the operation an evicted entry answered asks again and\n302:   no outcome is invented.\n303: \n304: On attach the link sends one `descriptor` request for the identity. Its\n305: answer is the reflection's confirmation. The `not-found` error marks the\n306: reflection **gone**; `ok` records the source's descriptor and declared\n307: surface. Either is appended to the event writer when one is composed.\n308: \n309: **Drain.** Every operation on any reflection first reads the link's channel\n310: reader to `blocked`, or until `drain-budget` reads (`ok` and `gap` alike),\n311: filing each answer whose id is outstanding, installing each\n312: `:dao.stream.remote/more` outcome at the cursor that precedes it, and\n313: filing each protocol error under its id. A drain the budget stops keeps\n314: the link's cursor where it stopped: this operation answers from what was\n315: filed so far, and the next operation's drain continues. At most the link's\n316: own budget `k` less one `more` outcomes are installed per answer, none when\n317: the link stamped no budget; a peer's surplus is dropped. Answers whose id\n318: is not outstanding are dropped. Non-answer values are dropped as\n319: diagnostics.\n320: This is where the asynchrony goes (`dao.stream.md`, Where the asynchrony\n321: goes): the caller's own polling is the cadence, and no driver step exists.\n322: \n323: Then the operation answers as follows.\n324: \n325: - `descriptor`: local. `ok` with the remote descriptor it was attached with\n326:   and `:dao.stream/identity`. Same identity as the source's own descriptor,\n327:   different reachability.\n328: - `cursor` `a`: a filed answer for `a` is returned, and a filed protocol\n329:   error is translated per the rules below. Otherwise a `cursor` request for"}],"deferred":false}}
{"type":"event","event":{"type":"tool_completed","toolCallId":"call_1cb1af990d334a068eda7a98","toolName":"read_file","result":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 135 lines\n1: # S2b independent review\n2: \n3: Verdict: **CHANGES REQUESTED**\n4: \n5: Reviewed the uncommitted diff against HEAD `e9645a2d` on branch\n6: `stream-crossmachine-s2`, exclusively in the dedicated worktree. Read the\n7: architect specification §1.2, §1.3, §2, §3 S2b and §4, the implementer report,\n8: and the architectural invariants. No code, test, or design files were edited.\n9: \n10: ## Blocking findings\n11: \n12: ### 1. P1 — Refused attach probes exceed max-outstanding and can deadlock retries\n13: \n14: Location: `src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:395–429`, particularly the unconditional\n15: pending insertion at lines 426–429; `retry-pending!` at lines 653–657.\n16: \n17: `send!` refuses a new probe when outstanding plus pending is at the cap, but\n18: `send-request!` then retains that refused probe in `:pending` anyway. Thus the\n19: configured retained-request bound is not enforced. Every additional attach\n20: retains another request and reflection. This directly contradicts spec §1.3\n21: (max entries in outstanding plus pending), the design documentation, and the\n22: claim that the retry scan is bounded by max-outstanding.\n23: \n24: It also prevents recovery, even with a healthy accepting writer. With\n25: max-outstanding 1, attach three reflections using the same `remote/links` value:\n26: there is one outstanding probe and two pending probes. Answer the first probe\n27: and drain repeatedly. Outstanding becomes zero, but both pending probes remain\n28: forever: each retry counts the other pending probe, sees count 1 at cap 1, and\n29: refuses itself. Ordinary new requests on the link are also refused. Closing\n30: reflections can free this state, but normal answering and polling cannot.\n31: \n32: Runtime reproduction using existing private test fixtures, loaded without\n33: modifying them, printed:\n34: \n35: ```text\n36: :after-attach 1 2\n37: :after-answer-and-drains 0 2\n38: ```\n39: \n40: Requested change: enforce the cap when retaining probes as well as when sending\n41: them, and provide an explicit recovery path for attachments that could not be\n42: admitted. Preserve confirmation retry semantics without keeping an unbounded\n43: queue or allowing pending entries to prevent all progress. Add tests for\n44: multiple attachments sharing a capped link, both an accepting writer and a\n45: writer returning full, and recovery after the first accepted answer.\n46: \n47: The report acknowledges pending growth as locally driven. That is not an\n48: exception in the architect contract, and it does not address the retry deadlock.\n49: \n50: ### 2. P1 — Prefetch eviction can make an ordinary next request permanently blocked\n51: \n52: Location: `src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:524–558` and `:625–628`.\n53: \n54: The primary answer is filed with age `[id 0]`, then its prefetched outcomes are\n55: installed with later ages. Eviction consequently discards the primary answer\n56: first. For valid policy `{:dao.stream.remote/max-filed 1\n57: :dao.stream.remote/budget 2}`, a healthy source with at least two readable\n58: values answers `next 0` with the value at 0 plus one prefetched outcome at 1.\n59: Every drain evicts the answer at 0 before the caller can consume it. Re-asking\n60: produces the same two entries and evicts the requested answer again.\n61: \n62: Runtime reproduction with `listed-reflection [:a :b :c]`, alternating\n63: `(stream/next r 0)` and `(serve! peer)`, printed:\n64: \n65: ```text\n66: :poll 0 #:dao.stream{:outcome :dao.stream/blocked}\n67: :poll 1 #:dao.stream{:outcome :dao.stream/blocked}\n68: :poll 2 #:dao.stream{:outcome :dao.stream/blocked}\n69: :poll 3 #:dao.stream{:outcome :dao.stream/blocked}\n70: :retained nil ([n 1])\n71: ```\n72: \n73: This repeats indefinitely with one consumer, no competing requests, no flood,\n74: and no transport failure. More generally it occurs when retained prefetch fills\n75: the cap and evicts its own primary answer. Thus eviction does not allow clean\n76: re-asking as required. The existing test deliberately expects a primary answer\n77: to disappear when budget 4 and max-filed 2 are combined, but does not check that\n78: the original read can subsequently succeed.\n79: \n80: Requested change: reconcile oldest-first eviction with progress for these valid\n81: policy combinations, for example by limiting requested/installed prefetch to\n82: the retention capacity so a fresh request's answer survives its own prefetch.\n83: Add a single-reader regression that actually retrieves the first value with\n84: max-filed 1 and budget 2, then advances normally. Any necessary change to the\n85: architect's stated eviction ordering should be made explicit; merely advising\n86: a larger cap leaves accepted configurations unusable.\n87: \n88: ## Contract assessment\n89: \n90: - Setup validation in `remote/links` correctly rejects non-positive integers\n91:   and invalid types for all three new keys via the specified ex-info data.\n92: - The drain loop bounds successful wire reads, retains the cursor, drops\n93:   unrelated values while counting them, and preserves the existing gap recovery\n94:   behavior. No fabricated end or source gap was introduced.\n95: - For ordinary cursor, next, resolve, and append requests at capacity, local\n96:   refusal follows the required writer-full outcome mappings and sends nothing.\n97:   Attach probe retention is the blocking exception above.\n98: - Filed entries and installed outcomes are counted together, with lowest-id\n99:   eviction and re-asking after eviction. Append answers remain emitted rather\n100:   than filed. The prefetch interaction is the blocking exception above.\n101: - The more clamp uses `budget - 1`, and installs none without a valid stamped\n102:   budget. This agrees with the architect's budget-2/one-installed test and the\n103:   existing mirror chase counting the primary answer as the first outcome. The\n104:   discrepancy with §1.3's prose saying at most budget is justified.\n105: - `ws-project/dial` passes all three link keys through its policy selection.\n106:   Design documentation covers the additions, but its hard retained-request\n107:   bound is not currently true.\n108: - No new global state, application callbacks, ambient clock, stream operation,\n109:   or source-outcome fabrication was found. State remains scoped to the existing\n110:   host-composed link/reflection boundary.\n111: \n112: ## Verification and coverage\n113: \n114: Independently ran:\n115: \n116: ```text\n117: clojure -M:test -n dao.stream.remote-test -n dao.stream.ws-project-test\n118: Ran 69 tests containing 537 assertions.\n119: 0 failures, 0 errors.\n120: ```\n121: \n122: Also executed both reproductions above with existing fixtures via `load-file`\n123: and expressions in `dao.stream.remote-test`; no test files were changed.\n124: \n125: The new tests establish basic draining, ordinary request refusal, consumption\n126: of retained answers before re-asking an evicted answer, clamping, and validation.\n127: They do not exercise pending-probe capacity/recovery or forward progress when\n128: prefetch exceeds retained capacity. Additional useful coverage is malformed\n129: wire values and gap continuation under a drain budget, and dial propagation of\n130: the new link options.\n131: \n132: The full JVM lane, Node/Dart lanes, and lint were not independently run in this\n133: review. The implementer's report also leaves the full lanes outstanding; the\n134: architect's §4 landing gates remain necessary after reconciliation. Passing the\n135: targeted suite does not resolve the two runtime-confirmed defects."}],"deferred":false}}
