e k]; min-key first → smallest age. ✓.\n\nNow the outstanding-for/filed-for filters: unchanged.\n\nAlso verify the `held` ≤ cap assertion at line 2020 of the second test: after `(stream/descriptor ra)`, held = outstanding {b} + pending {a} = 2 ≤ 2 ✓.\n\nAnother potential concern: in the second test, `full-then-forward-writer` rejects the first 3 appends. Attach order: a's probe (id 0) rejected → pending. b's probe (id 1): send! room? (0 + pending dissoc id1 = {a}=1 <2) → append → full (2nd) → room? true → pending={0,1}. c (id 2): room? 0+2=2 not <2 → local full, no append (writer not consumed) → room? false → :probe on c. So writer has 2 fulls consumed. `(stream/descriptor ra)`: drain → retry-pending!: send id 0: room? 0 + {1} =1 <2 → append → full (3rd time) → room? → re-retain in pending. Send id 1: room? 0 + {0} = 1 < 2 → append → forwards (n exhausted) → ok → register outstanding={1}, pending={0}. Then loop reads: nothing on :ba. admit-probe! ra: no :probe on ra. held = 1+1 = 2 ≤ 2 ✓.\n\n`(stream/descriptor rc)`: drain → retry-pending!: id 0: room? outstanding {1} =1 + pending dissoc 0 = 0 → 1 < 2 → append → ok → register outstanding={0,1}, pending={}. Read: nothing. admit-probe! rc: :probe → send-request! → send! room? 2 < 2 false → full → descriptor branch room? false → stays as :probe ✓. outstanding count = 2 ✓.\n\nserve! responds to ids 0 and 1 (mirror reads both requests from :ab — wait, requests went through the forwarding writer to (:ab t)? full-then-forward-writer wraps `(writer (:ab t))` = identity on :ab handle... `capped-reflections` passes `#(full-then-forward-writer % 3)`, and writer is applied to `(:ab t)`. After 3 rejections, forwards to ab. So ab has id 0's request (3rd append? no — 3rd append was rejected). Let me recount appends to the wrapper: 1) a-probe id0 → full. 2) b-probe id1 → full. 3) a-retry id0 → full. 4) b-retry id1 → forwarded. 5) a-retry (inside descriptor rc drain) id0 → forwarded. 6) c's admit attempt → not appended (local reject