{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I've got a comprehensive overall picture. Let me verify the two P1 fixes in the actual code and trace the logic carefully.\n\n**Fix 1: Capped probe retention + admit-probe!**\n\nCode:\n- `room?` (lines 408-418): `(< (+ (count outstanding) (count (dissoc pending id))) max-outstanding)` — excludes itself.\n- `send!` (lines 421-428): If there's no room, reject locally as full.\n- `send-request!` (lines 431-458): On ok — register, dissoc pending id, dissoc refl's :probe. On full + descriptor: if `(room? link id)` → put into `:pending` (with req and reflection) and dissoc refl's :probe; otherwise → `(swap! refl assoc :probe req)` — wait on its own reflection.\n- `admit-probe!` (lines 700-708): If refl has :probe and isn't closed and channel isn't gone → send-request!.\n- Called after drain! in refl-descriptor, refl-cursor, refl-next, refl-append (lines 762, 840, 864, 893).\n- `retry-pending!` (lines 691-697): Retries pending probes once per drain.\n- `refl-close` clears pending entries for this refl (lines 918-923), dissocs :probe (line 924).\n\nInvariant check: outstanding + pending ≤ max-outstanding.\n- New sends go through send!, need room? (excluding its own id — new requests aren't in pending, so dissoc is a no-op) → count outstanding + pending < max → after register, outstanding+1 ≤ max. OK.\n- Retaining a rejected probe into pending: only if room? — count outstanding + pending (excluding its own id; the probe isn't in pending yet so dissoc is no-op) < max → after assoc, outstanding + pending ≤ max. OK.\n- retry-pending!: send-request! with id already in pending. room? excludes its own id: outstanding + (pending - self) ≤ ... hmm, if the invariant outstanding + pending ≤ max holds, then outstanding + pending - self ≤ max - 1 < max → always room for send!. So send! actually attempts append. If the writer accepts → register (outstanding+1, pending-1 → total unchanged). If the writer rejects with full → room? is true → re-assoc into pending (already there). If room? is false — impossible for a probe already in pending. So the retry of a pending probe always has room and is only limited by the writer. Deadlock resolved.\n- Probes waiting on refl (:probe) don't count against the cap — the local caller's state is at most one per reflection. admit-probe! offers it again: send-request! → send! → if there's room now, append to writer; on ok register; on full with room → into pending; on no room → stays as :probe. OK.\n\nPossible subtle issue: In `send-request!`'s full+descriptor branch, `(room? link id)` — for a new attach probe, if room → pending. But what about the reflection's previous :probe? On the ok path it's dissoced. On the full path with room: assoc into pending and `(swap! refl dissoc :probe)`. On no-room: `(swap! refl assoc :probe req)`. No problem.\n\nWait — one subtle point: `send-request!`'s full+descriptor branch retains any descriptor request, not just attach probes. Is send-request! used for descriptor ops only from attach and retry-pending! and admit-probe!? Let me check: send-or-count! uses send-request! for cursor/next ops. Named descriptor requests in resolve-name go through send-named! not send-request!. So descriptor ops reaching send-request! are: only attach probes (from the links attach closure) and probe retries. OK.\n\nBut wait — what about full on cursor/next requests? send-request!'s cond only handles full for descriptor ops; otherwise returns r (full). send-or-count! ignores r and returns retry-read/blocked. Nothing is registered, request isn't retained → will be re-minted with a new id on next ask. That was the existing behavior. No problem.\n\nAnother subtle point: if the writer rejects with full in `send-request!` for a descriptor probe and `room?` is true, we put it into pending with an already-minted id. `mint-id!` incremented. Later when retry-pending! sends it, same id. On ok, register!. No problem.\n\nDeadlock check from round 1: max-outstanding 1, 3 attaches. Attach a: send! → room (0<1) → append ok → register, outstanding={a}. Attach b: send! → room? outstanding=1, pending=0 → 1<1 false → full. Then descriptor branch: room? false → :probe on refl b. Attach c similarly → :probe on refl c. pending={}, outstanding={a}. held=1. Test expects this. serve! responds to a → drain: outstanding={}. Then `(stream/descriptor rb)`: drain!, admit-probe! rb → send-request! → send! → room (0<1) → append → register → outstanding={b}, probes on wire=2. Test expects 2. Then `(stream/descriptor rc)`: drain (no answer to b yet), admit-probe! rc → send! → room? outstanding=1 → 1<1 false → full → room? false → stays as :probe. Probes still 2. held=1. serve! responds to b. `(stream/descriptor rc)`: drain absorbs b's answer (outstanding={}), admit-probe! rc → send → probes=3. serve!, descriptor rc → drain absorbs c → all confirmed, held=0. Matches the test.\n\nRound 1's deadlock scenario is resolved: pending probes never exceed cap, and probes without room wait on their own reflection rather than pending, so there's no mutual counting.\n\nTrade-off documented: reflections that are never operated on are never confirmed. Acceptable, matches the doc.\n\nSecond test scenario: cap 2, writer rejects the first 3 appends with full (full-then-forward-writer 3). Attach a: send! → room? outstanding 0 + pending 0 < 2 → yes → append → writer responds full (1st time). Descriptor branch: room? still true (0<2) → pending={a}. Attach b: send! → room? outstanding 0 + pending (dissoc b → still {a}) = 1 < 2 → append → full (2nd time) → room? 1<2 → pending={a,b}. Attach c: send! → room? 0 + 2 = 2 < 2 false → locally full → room? false → :probe on c. Test: pending count=2 ✓, rc has :probe ✓.\n\n`(stream/descriptor ra)`: drain! → retry-pending! → for each pending entry, send-request!. Order of pending (hash map, but keys are id 0,1). Send a: send! → room? outstanding 0 + pending dissoc a = {b} = 1 < 2 → append → writer: 3rd full → full. Descriptor branch: room? true → re-assoc into pending (already there), dissoc :probe (none). Send b: room? outstanding 0 + pending dissoc b = {a} = 1 < 2 → append → writer now forwards → ok → register! outstanding={b}, pending dissoc b → pending={a}. Hmm wait — but the writer was full-then-forward-writer with n=3: first 3 appends rejected. Appends so far: a (full), b (full), a-retry (full) — that's 3. b-retry → forwarded → ok. So outstanding={b}, pending={a}. drain continues to read — nothing. admit-probe! ra — ra has no :probe (never set; a went into pending). held = 1+1 = 2 ≤ 2 ✓.\n\n`(stream/descriptor rc)`: drain → retry-pending!: send a: room? outstanding={b}=1 + pending dissoc a = 0 → 1 < 2 → append → forwarded ok → register → outstanding={a,b}, pending={}. Then read loop: no answers yet (peer hasn't served). admit-probe! rc: :probe on rc → send-request! → send! → room? outstanding=2 → 2<2 false → full → descriptor branch → room? false → stays as :probe. Test: outstanding count=2 ✓, rc has :probe ✓.\n\nserve! → mirror responds to both a and b. `(stream/descriptor rc)`: drain absorbs both answers → outstanding={}, both confirmed... wait, a's probe response confirms ra, b's confirms rb. admit-probe! rc → room → send → ok, outstanding={c}, rc's :probe dissoced → nil ✓. serve!, descriptor rc → absorb → all confirmed, held=0 ✓.\n\nTest logic is sound.\n\n**Fix 2: Prefetch never evicts its own answer**\n\n- `stamped-budget` (lines 384-392): min(budget, max-filed) if both set.\n- `wire-request` stamps next requests with it.\n- `install-more!` (lines 580-606): asked = k-1 (k = stamped budget); fits = max-filed − filed − filed-cursors (or asked if no cap); installs min(asked, fits).\n- `file!` files with the latest :age from `next-age!` (monotonic counter), then `evict-filed!` evicts from the oldest age. So the answer just filed is the newest, and eviction removes older entries first — never itself.\n\nRound 1 defect: max-filed 1, budget 2.