before its mores (age n+1). Hmm, that's fine for the \"never evicts its own answer at file time\" property; later evictions follow arrival order, which is the documented semantics.\n\nEdge: `stamped-budget` when budget is nil → returns nil → wire-request doesn't stamp budget → install-more!'s asked = 0 (since k isn't a positive integer). Matches doc: \"none if the link didn't stamp a budget\".\n\nEdge: budget set, max-filed nil → stamped = budget, fits = asked. Same as old behavior.\n\n`more-is-clamped-to-the-links-own-budget` third row: budget 4, max-filed 2 → stamped = 2, asked = 1. Test injects response with :more of 5 outcomes. file! files answer (filed=1, age 0; no eviction since 1 ≤ 2). install-more!: fits = 2 − 1 − 0 = 1, min(asked=1, fits=1) = 1 → installs 1. Test expects filed=1, installed=1 ✓ (old defective expectation was filed 0, installed 2 — reconcile says updated; current test shows `1 1`).\n\nHmm wait, in that test `(stream/next r 0)` sends the request, then appends a forged response with id 1 directly to :ba, then `(stream/descriptor r)` drains. But the attach probe also has id... probe is id 0, next request is id 1. Forged response has id 1 ✓. Probe response never arrives — probe remains outstanding. file! requires the response id to be outstanding — id 1 is outstanding ✓. But wait, drain absorbs values in order; forged response has :more vector — well-formed-answer? requires outcome-map?; response has outcome ok, value, cursor, more. Assuming outcome-map? allows extra keys. This is the existing pattern.\n\nNote: install-more! reads `(:dao.stream.remote/more answer)` — takes (min asked fits) = 1 element and installs at [identity, cursor-of-answer]. identity is \"n\" ✓.\n\n**ws-project changes**: dial passes max-outstanding/max-filed via select-keys in policy (lines 481-486) ✓ — round 1 review confirmed. make-acceptor's mirror-bounds etc. were S0/S1.\n\n**Remaining concerns / regression hazards:**\n\n1. `count-ask!`'s resend path (line 473): `(stream/append! (:writer @link) (:req entry))` — bypasses send!/room?! Resending an outstanding request doesn't add to outstanding count (same id already counted), so the cap outstanding+pending isn't violated. But it does bypass the writer... just appends; if the writer is full, the resend is silently dropped and asks is reset to 0 — existing behavior from before S2b? Let me check if this existed. This is S2a-era resend-after logic. Cap invariant counts retained requests not wire writes, so no violation. OK.\n\n2. `resolve-name`'s send-named! uses send! (with room check) ✓; named response is filed via file! with reflection nil — filed-for requires (= refl (:reflection e))... named lookup uses named-id not filed-for ✓.\n\n3. admit-probe! after drain in refl-append, even if append is rejected — no problem.\n\n4. `refl-close` removes pending probes for that refl and dissocs :probe ✓. But channel-loss!: outstanding discarded; what about pending? On channel-gone, retry-pending!: drain! doesn't run when channel-gone? (when-not guard). admit-probe! checks channel-gone? ✓. Pending probes remain in :pending after channel loss — but they're bounded, and close! removes them. Minor, existing pattern (channel-loss! doesn't clear pending either). Not a regression from this diff? Actually, :pending was introduced in S2b. Hmm — after channel-gone?, pending probes stay forever until refl close. Memory is bounded by max-outstanding. Minor note, not blocking.\n\n5. Race / atomicity: multiple swap!s on link atom aren't atomic across steps (e.g., send-request! does send! then register! then dissoc pending). Design assumes driver solo/single-threaded stepping (\"driver alone steps\"). Consistent with existing code. Not a regression.\n\n6. `room?` reads @link once; send! then appends. Between room? and register!, count doesn't change from elsewhere (single-threaded). OK.\n\n7. In `retry-pending!`, doseq over snapshot of `(:pending @link)` while send-request! mutates pending — doseq holds the snapshot so mutation during iteration is safe. If a pending probe sends ok, it's dissoced; later entry iterations unaffected. But: could retry-pending! send the same probe twice if both :pending and refl's :probe refer to it? send-request! on full+no-room sets :probe but leaves... wait — can a probe be in both pending and refl's :probe? Path: probe rejected with room → pending (and :probe dissoced). Later retry-pending! → send-request! → if now no room: send! rejects → descriptor branch → room? false → assoc :probe on refl — but it's still in pending! Then admit-probe! also offers it → send-request! again → potential double-send if writer accepts? Let me trace: probe in pending={id}, and refl's :probe=req (same req). retry-pending! on next drain: send-request! → room? id excludes self from pending: outstanding + (pending−self). If there's room → append → on ok: register!, dissoc pending id, dissoc :probe → both cleaned. If full (writer) → room? true → re-assoc into pending, dissoc :probe → cleaned. If no room → assoc :probe (already there). Then admit-probe! (after drain, in refl op): :probe is still set → send-request! → same flow. Could both retry-pending! and admit-probe! successfully append the same probe in one drain? Sequence within one refl op: drain! (runs retry-pending! then reads) → admit-probe!. If retry-pending! successfully sends the probe, on ok it dissocs both pending and :probe → admit-probe! sees nil :probe → does nothing. If retry-pending!'s send was rejected by writer with full → room? true → pending retains, :probe dissoced → admit-probe! does nothing. So double-send requires retry-pending! to leave :probe set and pending set (the no-room path), then room appearing before admit-probe! — but admit-probe! runs in the same op, right after drain!, with no intervening state change... actually drain!'s read loop absorbs answers after retry-pending! runs (retry-pending! is at line 729 before the loop at line 730). Absorbing answers removes outstanding entries → room appears! Then admit-probe! → send-request! → send! → room → append → ok → register, dissoc pending, dissoc :probe. Single send ✓. But the probe is in both pending and... after this send, both are cleared. What about the next drain's retry-pending!? — pending no longer has it. OK.\n\nBut another interleaving: probe in pending (retry-pending! sent it with no room → :probe also set). Then admit-probe! sends it ok → register!, dissoc pending, dissoc :probe. No problem.\n\nCan admit-probe! and retry-pending! both append in the same drain? retry-pending! runs first; if its append succeeded, :probe is dissoced and admit-probe! does nothing. If it failed, no append happened. So no double-append in one drain. Across drains: if append succeeded, both markers cleared. Only danger: append ok but register! is... no, single-threaded.\n\nHmm wait, actually one more: retry-pending! succeeds append → ok → dissoc :probe — but what if the probe was set on refl by a different req? :probe only holds that refl's own descriptor probe; pending entries are keyed by id with :reflection. Same probe → same id. If admit-probe! sent probe P (id 5) ok, and an older... :probe only holds one req per refl, and pending id 5 is dissoced. Could refl's :probe hold a req with a different id than the pending entry? Attach mints id once per attach; send-request! reuses the same req map on retry. admit-probe! passes the same req. So same id. Only scenario for different ids: attach → probe rejected → :probe (id 2). Then... nothing mints another probe for the same refl. OK, no dupes.\n\n8. `held` test helper counts outstanding + pending — matches cap invariant.\n\n9. `filed-next!` dissocs filed entry without evicting anything — no problem.\n\n10. One more check — `install-more!` uses `stamped-budget` at install time reading current link state; budget/max-filed are immutable policy. OK.\n\n11. Doc consistency: lines 289-293 match implementation ✓; lines 294-299 ✓.\n\n**Test execution**: Cannot run