r :closable} — has :reader ✓. filed-next! nil → send next request id 1, stamped budget = min(2,1)=1 → blocked ✓.\n- Wire budget check: first next request's budget = 1 ✓.\n- Loop c=0 v=:a: c=0 not pos → skip extra blocked check. serve!: mirror responds to next 1 with budget 1: outcome ok :a cursor 1, chase k=1 → n=1 ≥ k → more=[] → no :more. `(stream/next r 0)`: drain absorbs → file! (filed={1}, age 0; evict: 1 ≤ 1 ok) → filed-next! finds filed-for refl next 0... wait — filed-for matches `(= arg (first args))` where arg is c=0. Request's args are [0] ✓ → returns :a, dissoc filed. Retained = 0 ≤ 1 ✓.\n- c=1: pos → `(stream/next r 1)`: filed-next! nil → send id 2 → blocked ✓ \"nothing prefetched beyond retained capacity\" (indeed no prefetch was installed). serve! → responds with :b cursor 2. `(stream/next r 1)` → :b ✓. Retained ≤1 ✓.\n- c=2 similarly → :c ✓.\n- Final serve!: no pending requests (id 3 not yet sent). `(stream/next r 3)`: sends id 3, blocked ✓. Message says \"source's own blocked at its end\" — well, reflection's blocked; source should also respond with blocked. Minor phrasing in test message, not a defect.\n\nAlso check `mirror-step`'s chase clamp with budget 1: k=1, `(>= n k)` with n=1 → more=[]. ✓.\n\nOne more check — `wire-request` only stamps budget on next ops. `install-more!`'s asked = stamped−1 = 0 (with budget 2/max-filed 1) → even if peer maliciously sends :more, nothing installed (take 0). ✓.\n\nPotential regression hazard: stamped-budget clamps budget to max-filed — what if max-filed is 1 and budget is 4? stamped=1 → prefetch entirely disabled. That's documented (\"stamped budget is at most max-filed\"). Reasonable: with m=1 you can't retain prefetch anyway.\n\nWhat about max-filed = 3, budget = 2? stamped = 2, asked = 1, fits up to 3. No problem.\n\nVerify evict-filed! loop termination: each iteration removes one entry; total is finite → terminates. min-key on non-empty aged — aged is non-empty when total > m ≥ 1. ✓.\n\n`(apply min-key first