{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_end","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I've got a comprehensive overall picture. Let me verify the two P1 fixes in the actual code and trace the logic carefully.\n\n**Fix 1: Capped probe retention + admit-probe!**\n\nCode:\n- `room?` (lines 408-418): `(< (+ (count outstanding) (count (dissoc pending id))) max-outstanding)` — excludes itself.\n- `send!` (lines 421-428): If there's no room, reject locally as full.\n- `send-request!` (lines 431-458): On ok — register, dissoc pending id, dissoc refl's :probe. On full + descriptor: if `(room? link id)` → put into `:pending` (with req and reflection) and dissoc refl's :probe; otherwise → `(swap! refl assoc :probe req)` — wait on its own reflection.\n- `admit-probe!` (lines 700-708): If refl has :probe and isn't closed and channel isn't gone → send-request!.\n- Called after drain! in refl-descriptor, refl-cursor, refl-next, refl-append (lines 762, 840, 864, 893).\n- `retry-pending!` (lines 691-697): Retries pending probes once per drain.\n- `refl-close` clears pending entries for this refl (lines 918-923), dissocs :probe (line 924).\n\nInvariant check: outstanding + pending ≤ max-outstanding.\n- New sends go through send!, need room? (excluding its own id — new requests aren't in pending, so dissoc is a no-op) → count outstanding + pending < max → after register, outstanding+1 ≤ max. OK.\n- Retaining a rejected probe into pending: only if room? — count outstanding + pending (excluding its own id; the probe isn't in pending yet so dissoc is no-op) < max → after assoc, outstanding + pending ≤ max. OK.\n- retry-pending!: send-request! with id already in pending. room? excludes its own id: outstanding + (pending - self) ≤ ... hmm, if the invariant outstanding + pending ≤ max holds, then outstanding + pending - self ≤ max - 1 < max → always room for send!. So send! actually attempts append. If the writer accepts → register (outstanding+1, pending-1 → total unchanged). If the writer rejects with full → room? is true → re-assoc into pending (already there). If room? is false — impossible for a probe already in pending. So the retry of a pending probe always has room and is only limited by the writer. Deadlock resolved.\n- Probes waiting on refl (:probe) don't count against the cap — the local caller's state is at most one per reflection. admit-probe! offers it again: send-request! → send! → if there's room now, append to writer; on ok register; on full with room → into pending; on no room → stays as :probe. OK.\n\nPossible subtle issue: In `send-request!`'s full+descriptor branch, `(room? link id)` — for a new attach probe, if room → pending. But what about the reflection's previous :probe? On the ok path it's dissoced. On the full path with room: assoc into pending and `(swap! refl dissoc :probe)`. On no-room: `(swap! refl assoc :probe req)`. No problem.\n\nWait — one subtle point: `send-request!`'s full+descriptor branch retains any descriptor request, not just attach probes. Is send-request! used for descriptor ops only from attach and retry-pending! and admit-probe!? Let me check: send-or-count! uses send-request! for cursor/next ops. Named descriptor requests in resolve-name go through send-named! not send-request!. So descriptor ops reaching send-request! are: only attach probes (from the links attach closure) and probe retries. OK.\n\nBut wait — what about full on cursor/next requests? send-request!'s cond only handles full for descriptor ops; otherwise returns r (full). send-or-count! ignores r and returns retry-read/blocked. Nothing is registered, request isn't retained → will be re-minted with a new id on next ask. That was the existing behavior. No problem.\n\nAnother subtle point: if the writer rejects with full in `send-request!` for a descriptor probe and `room?` is true, we put it into pending with an already-minted id. `mint-id!` incremented. Later when retry-pending! sends it, same id. On ok, register!. No problem.\n\nDeadlock check from round 1: max-outstanding 1, 3 attaches. Attach a: send! → room (0<1) → append ok → register, outstanding={a}. Attach b: send! → room? outstanding=1, pending=0 → 1<1 false → full. Then descriptor branch: room? false → :probe on refl b. Attach c similarly → :probe on refl c. pending={}, outstanding={a}. held=1. Test expects this. serve! responds to a → drain: outstanding={}. Then `(stream/descriptor rb)`: drain!, admit-probe! rb → send-request! → send! → room (0<1) → append → register → outstanding={b}, probes on wire=2. Test expects 2. Then `(stream/descriptor rc)`: drain (no answer to b yet), admit-probe! rc → send! → room? outstanding=1 → 1<1 false → full → room? false → stays as :probe. Probes still 2. held=1. serve! responds to b. `(stream/descriptor rc)`: drain absorbs b's answer (outstanding={}), admit-probe! rc → send → probes=3. serve!, descriptor rc → drain absorbs c → all confirmed, held=0. Matches the test.\n\nRound 1's deadlock scenario is resolved: pending probes never exceed cap, and probes without room wait on their own reflection rather than pending, so there's no mutual counting.\n\nTrade-off documented: reflections that are never operated on are never confirmed. Acceptable, matches the doc.\n\nSecond test scenario: cap 2, writer rejects the first 3 appends with full (full-then-forward-writer 3). Attach a: send! → room? outstanding 0 + pending 0 < 2 → yes → append → writer responds full (1st time). Descriptor branch: room? still true (0<2) → pending={a}. Attach b: send! → room? outstanding 0 + pending (dissoc b → still {a}) = 1 < 2 → append → full (2nd time) → room? 1<2 → pending={a,b}. Attach c: send! → room? 0 + 2 = 2 < 2 false → locally full → room? false → :probe on c. Test: pending count=2 ✓, rc has :probe ✓.\n\n`(stream/descriptor ra)`: drain! → retry-pending! → for each pending entry, send-request!. Order of pending (hash map, but keys are id 0,1). Send a: send! → room? outstanding 0 + pending dissoc a = {b} = 1 < 2 → append → writer: 3rd full → full. Descriptor branch: room? true → re-assoc into pending (already there), dissoc :probe (none). Send b: room? outstanding 0 + pending dissoc b = {a} = 1 < 2 → append → writer now forwards → ok → register! outstanding={b}, pending dissoc b → pending={a}. Hmm wait — but the writer was full-then-forward-writer with n=3: first 3 appends rejected. Appends so far: a (full), b (full), a-retry (full) — that's 3. b-retry → forwarded → ok. So outstanding={b}, pending={a}. drain continues to read — nothing. admit-probe! ra — ra has no :probe (never set; a went into pending). held = 1+1 = 2 ≤ 2 ✓.\n\n`(stream/descriptor rc)`: drain → retry-pending!: send a: room? outstanding={b}=1 + pending dissoc a = 0 → 1 < 2 → append → forwarded ok → register → outstanding={a,b}, pending={}. Then read loop: no answers yet (peer hasn't served). admit-probe! rc: :probe on rc → send-request! → send! → room? outstanding=2 → 2<2 false → full → descriptor branch → room? false → stays as :probe. Test: outstanding count=2 ✓, rc has :probe ✓.\n\nserve! → mirror responds to both a and b. `(stream/descriptor rc)`: drain absorbs both answers → outstanding={}, both confirmed... wait, a's probe response confirms ra, b's confirms rb. admit-probe! rc → room → send → ok, outstanding={c}, rc's :probe dissoced → nil ✓. serve!, descriptor rc → absorb → all confirmed, held=0 ✓.\n\nTest logic is sound.\n\n**Fix 2: Prefetch never evicts its own answer**\n\n- `stamped-budget` (lines 384-392): min(budget, max-filed) if both set.\n- `wire-request` stamps next requests with it.\n- `install-more!` (lines 580-606): asked = k-1 (k = stamped budget); fits = max-filed − filed − filed-cursors (or asked if no cap); installs min(asked, fits).\n- `file!` files with the latest :age from `next-age!` (monotonic counter), then `evict-filed!` evicts from the oldest age. So the answer just filed is the newest, and eviction removes older entries first — never itself.\n\nRound 1 defect: max-filed 1, budget 2. stamped = min(2,1) = 1. asked = 0. So no prefetch is installed at all. Wire budget is 1 → mirror doesn't chase → no :more. file! files answer, evict: filed=1 ≤ 1, no eviction. next returns it. Test checks stamped budget on wire = 1 ✓, each read progresses ✓, retained ≤ 1 ✓.\n\nEven if the peer sends more (excess), install-more! clamps to fits = 1 − 1 − 0 = 0 → installs nothing. Double protection.\n\nEviction order change: from lowest id to arrival order via :filed-seq counter. The reconcile report flags this as an explicit change from the architect's \"lowest id\" ordering, with legitimate reason: a re-asked answer has a lower id than newer retained entries, and lowest-id eviction would evict a newly filed answer — same starvation. Doc updated: \"in the order the link retained them, so filing an answer never evicts that answer\" (lines 295-297). Doc §2.4 also states the stamped budget is at most max-filed (line 299) ✓.\n\nCheck `evict-filed!`: loop while total > m, evict min age. `file!` files first (with latest age), then evicts → never evicts itself unless m=0... m is positive integer or nil (validated by links). In the case m=1 with one old filed and one old filed-cursor: total=2 >1 → evict oldest. No problem.\n\nCheck `install-more!` interaction with eviction: installs with new age after file!. file!'s eviction runs before install-more!. So after install, total ≤ max? file! ensures filed ≤ m... actually file!+evict ensures filed+filed-cursors ≤ m. Then install-more! computes fits = m − filed − filed-cursors ≥ 0, installs at most fits → total ≤ m. No eviction needed after install. Good.\n\nabsorb!'s ordering (lines 671-672): `(file! link id req v refl)` followed by `(install-more! link v)`. Good — answer is filed first with older age than its installed mores... wait, file! gives the answer age n, install-more! gives mores n+1... if the cap forces eviction later, mores are evicted before the answer? No — eviction is oldest-first, so the answer (age n) is evicted before its mores (age n+1). Hmm, that's fine for the \"never evicts its own answer at file time\" property; later evictions follow arrival order, which is the documented semantics.\n\nEdge: `stamped-budget` when budget is nil → returns nil → wire-request doesn't stamp budget → install-more!'s asked = 0 (since k isn't a positive integer). Matches doc: \"none if the link didn't stamp a budget\".\n\nEdge: budget set, max-filed nil → stamped = budget, fits = asked. Same as old behavior.\n\n`more-is-clamped-to-the-links-own-budget` third row: budget 4, max-filed 2 → stamped = 2, asked = 1. Test injects response with :more of 5 outcomes. file! files answer (filed=1, age 0; no eviction since 1 ≤ 2). install-more!: fits = 2 − 1 − 0 = 1, min(asked=1, fits=1) = 1 → installs 1. Test expects filed=1, installed=1 ✓ (old defective expectation was filed 0, installed 2 — reconcile says updated; current test shows `1 1`).\n\nHmm wait, in that test `(stream/next r 0)` sends the request, then appends a forged response with id 1 directly to :ba, then `(stream/descriptor r)` drains. But the attach probe also has id... probe is id 0, next request is id 1. Forged response has id 1 ✓. Probe response never arrives — probe remains outstanding. file! requires the response id to be outstanding — id 1 is outstanding ✓. But wait, drain absorbs values in order; forged response has :more vector — well-formed-answer? requires outcome-map?; response has outcome ok, value, cursor, more. Assuming outcome-map? allows extra keys. This is the existing pattern.\n\nNote: install-more! reads `(:dao.stream.remote/more answer)` — takes (min asked fits) = 1 element and installs at [identity, cursor-of-answer]. identity is \"n\" ✓.\n\n**ws-project changes**: dial passes max-outstanding/max-filed via select-keys in policy (lines 481-486) ✓ — round 1 review confirmed. make-acceptor's mirror-bounds etc. were S0/S1.\n\n**Remaining concerns / regression hazards:**\n\n1. `count-ask!`'s resend path (line 473): `(stream/append! (:writer @link) (:req entry))` — bypasses send!/room?! Resending an outstanding request doesn't add to outstanding count (same id already counted), so the cap outstanding+pending isn't violated. But it does bypass the writer... just appends; if the writer is full, the resend is silently dropped and asks is reset to 0 — existing behavior from before S2b? Let me check if this existed. This is S2a-era resend-after logic. Cap invariant counts retained requests not wire writes, so no violation. OK.\n\n2. `resolve-name`'s send-named! uses send! (with room check) ✓; named response is filed via file! with reflection nil — filed-for requires (= refl (:reflection e))... named lookup uses named-id not filed-for ✓.\n\n3. admit-probe! after drain in refl-append, even if append is rejected — no problem.\n\n4. `refl-close` removes pending probes for that refl and dissocs :probe ✓. But channel-loss!: outstanding discarded; what about pending? On channel-gone, retry-pending!: drain! doesn't run when channel-gone? (when-not guard). admit-probe! checks channel-gone? ✓. Pending probes remain in :pending after channel loss — but they're bounded, and close! removes them. Minor, existing pattern (channel-loss! doesn't clear pending either). Not a regression from this diff? Actually, :pending was introduced in S2b. Hmm — after channel-gone?, pending probes stay forever until refl close. Memory is bounded by max-outstanding. Minor note, not blocking.\n\n5. Race / atomicity: multiple swap!s on link atom aren't atomic across steps (e.g., send-request! does send! then register! then dissoc pending). Design assumes driver solo/single-threaded stepping (\"driver alone steps\"). Consistent with existing code. Not a regression.\n\n6. `room?` reads @link once; send! then appends. Between room? and register!, count doesn't change from elsewhere (single-threaded). OK.\n\n7. In `retry-pending!`, doseq over snapshot of `(:pending @link)` while send-request! mutates pending — doseq holds the snapshot so mutation during iteration is safe. If a pending probe sends ok, it's dissoced; later entry iterations unaffected. But: could retry-pending! send the same probe twice if both :pending and refl's :probe refer to it? send-request! on full+no-room sets :probe but leaves... wait — can a probe be in both pending and refl's :probe? Path: probe rejected with room → pending (and :probe dissoced). Later retry-pending! → send-request! → if now no room: send! rejects → descriptor branch → room? false → assoc :probe on refl — but it's still in pending! Then admit-probe! also offers it → send-request! again → potential double-send if writer accepts? Let me trace: probe in pending={id}, and refl's :probe=req (same req). retry-pending! on next drain: send-request! → room? id excludes self from pending: outstanding + (pending−self). If there's room → append → on ok: register!, dissoc pending id, dissoc :probe → both cleaned. If full (writer) → room? true → re-assoc into pending, dissoc :probe → cleaned. If no room → assoc :probe (already there). Then admit-probe! (after drain, in refl op): :probe is still set → send-request! → same flow. Could both retry-pending! and admit-probe! successfully append the same probe in one drain? Sequence within one refl op: drain! (runs retry-pending! then reads) → admit-probe!. If retry-pending! successfully sends the probe, on ok it dissocs both pending and :probe → admit-probe! sees nil :probe → does nothing. If retry-pending!'s send was rejected by writer with full → room? true → pending retains, :probe dissoced → admit-probe! does nothing. So double-send requires retry-pending! to leave :probe set and pending set (the no-room path), then room appearing before admit-probe! — but admit-probe! runs in the same op, right after drain!, with no intervening state change... actually drain!'s read loop absorbs answers after retry-pending! runs (retry-pending! is at line 729 before the loop at line 730). Absorbing answers removes outstanding entries → room appears! Then admit-probe! → send-request! → send! → room → append → ok → register, dissoc pending, dissoc :probe. Single send ✓. But the probe is in both pending and... after this send, both are cleared. What about the next drain's retry-pending!? — pending no longer has it. OK.\n\nBut another interleaving: probe in pending (retry-pending! sent it with no room → :probe also set). Then admit-probe! sends it ok → register!, dissoc pending, dissoc :probe. No problem.\n\nCan admit-probe! and retry-pending! both append in the same drain? retry-pending! runs first; if its append succeeded, :probe is dissoced and admit-probe! does nothing. If it failed, no append happened. So no double-append in one drain. Across drains: if append succeeded, both markers cleared. Only danger: append ok but register! is... no, single-threaded.\n\nHmm wait, actually one more: retry-pending! succeeds append → ok → dissoc :probe — but what if the probe was set on refl by a different req? :probe only holds that refl's own descriptor probe; pending entries are keyed by id with :reflection. Same probe → same id. If admit-probe! sent probe P (id 5) ok, and an older... :probe only holds one req per refl, and pending id 5 is dissoced. Could refl's :probe hold a req with a different id than the pending entry? Attach mints id once per attach; send-request! reuses the same req map on retry. admit-probe! passes the same req. So same id. Only scenario for different ids: attach → probe rejected → :probe (id 2). Then... nothing mints another probe for the same refl. OK, no dupes.\n\n8. `held` test helper counts outstanding + pending — matches cap invariant.\n\n9. `filed-next!` dissocs filed entry without evicting anything — no problem.\n\n10. One more check — `install-more!` uses `stamped-budget` at install time reading current link state; budget/max-filed are immutable policy. OK.\n\n11. Doc consistency: lines 289-293 match implementation ✓; lines 294-299 ✓.\n\n**Test execution**: Cannot run — shell blocked (\"Tool requires permissions... use --yolo\"). I'm in plan mode too; running the test suite is close to read-only but generates build artifacts, and the shell tool is completely gated in this session. This must be reported honestly: couldn't independently execute in this session; the implementer's reconcile report claims 71 tests / 565 assertions passing; round 1 reviewer measured 69/537 before the two new tests were added — count delta consistent (2 new deftests added). \n\nActually wait — in plan mode, running tests via shell_command is blocked by permissions regardless. Should I try again? The error says requires --yolo. Retrying won't help.\n\nHmm, but the task says \"run clojure -M:test...\". Cannot. Will document.\n\n**Where to write the report**: task says write to collab/1791357700000-stream-s2b-review-r2.qwen3.8-max.findings.md. Plan mode: cannot write outside ~/.commandcode/plans/. Write full findings to a plan file and note that after exiting plan mode, the report should be copied to the collab path. Actually — best approach: write the review report content itself as the plan file (plan = report + steps to place it and run tests). Let's do that.\n\nAlso, verify the assertion count claim: cannot. And check cljstyle/kondo claims: cannot.\n\nLet me also double-check the `a-prefetch-never-evicts-its-own-answer` test's final `(serve! peer)` and `(stream/next r 3)` → blocked. list-stream [:a :b :c]: next at 3 → source is blocked. But reflection's next 3: not filed → send request → blocked. ✓. Wait — before that, after reading :c at cursor 2, the response to next 2 gave cursor 3. Final serve! handles any leftover requests... loop iteration for c=2: `(stream/next r 2)` when pos? — c=2 is pos → assert blocked (sends request for 2)... let me re-trace:\n\nlisted-reflection: attach probe id 0, serve!, descriptor → drained, probe confirmed. filed={}.\n- `(stream/next r 0)`: drain (nothing), admit-probe (none), not closed/gone, surface learned #{:reader :closable} — has :reader ✓. filed-next! nil → send next request id 1, stamped budget = min(2,1)=1 → blocked ✓.\n- Wire budget check: first next request's budget = 1 ✓.\n- Loop c=0 v=:a: c=0 not pos → skip extra blocked check. serve!: mirror responds to next 1 with budget 1: outcome ok :a cursor 1, chase k=1 → n=1 ≥ k → more=[] → no :more. `(stream/next r 0)`: drain absorbs → file! (filed={1}, age 0; evict: 1 ≤ 1 ok) → filed-next! finds filed-for refl next 0... wait — filed-for matches `(= arg (first args))` where arg is c=0. Request's args are [0] ✓ → returns :a, dissoc filed. Retained = 0 ≤ 1 ✓.\n- c=1: pos → `(stream/next r 1)`: filed-next! nil → send id 2 → blocked ✓ \"nothing prefetched beyond retained capacity\" (indeed no prefetch was installed). serve! → responds with :b cursor 2. `(stream/next r 1)` → :b ✓. Retained ≤1 ✓.\n- c=2 similarly → :c ✓.\n- Final serve!: no pending requests (id 3 not yet sent). `(stream/next r 3)`: sends id 3, blocked ✓. Message says \"source's own blocked at its end\" — well, reflection's blocked; source should also respond with blocked. Minor phrasing in test message, not a defect.\n\nAlso check `mirror-step`'s chase clamp with budget 1: k=1, `(>= n k)` with n=1 → more=[]. ✓.\n\nOne more check — `wire-request` only stamps budget on next ops. `install-more!`'s asked = stamped−1 = 0 (with budget 2/max-filed 1) → even if peer maliciously sends :more, nothing installed (take 0). ✓.\n\nPotential regression hazard: stamped-budget clamps budget to max-filed — what if max-filed is 1 and budget is 4? stamped=1 → prefetch entirely disabled. That's documented (\"stamped budget is at most max-filed\"). Reasonable: with m=1 you can't retain prefetch anyway.\n\nWhat about max-filed = 3, budget = 2? stamped = 2, asked = 1, fits up to 3. No problem.\n\nVerify evict-filed! loop termination: each iteration removes one entry; total is finite → terminates. min-key on non-empty aged — aged is non-empty when total > m ≥ 1. ✓.\n\n`(apply min-key first aged)` — aged entries are [age where k]; min-key first → smallest age. ✓.\n\nNow the outstanding-for/filed-for filters: unchanged.\n\nAlso verify the `held` ≤ cap assertion at line 2020 of the second test: after `(stream/descriptor ra)`, held = outstanding {b} + pending {a} = 2 ≤ 2 ✓.\n\nAnother potential concern: in the second test, `full-then-forward-writer` rejects the first 3 appends. Attach order: a's probe (id 0) rejected → pending. b's probe (id 1): send! room? (0 + pending dissoc id1 = {a}=1 <2) → append → full (2nd) → room? true → pending={0,1}. c (id 2): room? 0+2=2 not <2 → local full, no append (writer not consumed) → room? false → :probe on c. So writer has 2 fulls consumed. `(stream/descriptor ra)`: drain → retry-pending!: send id 0: room? 0 + {1} =1 <2 → append → full (3rd time) → room? → re-retain in pending. Send id 1: room? 0 + {0} = 1 < 2 → append → forwards (n exhausted) → ok → register outstanding={1}, pending={0}. Then loop reads: nothing on :ba. admit-probe! ra: no :probe on ra. held = 1+1 = 2 ≤ 2 ✓.\n\n`(stream/descriptor rc)`: drain → retry-pending!: id 0: room? outstanding {1} =1 + pending dissoc 0 = 0 → 1 < 2 → append → ok → register outstanding={0,1}, pending={}. Read: nothing. admit-probe! rc: :probe → send-request! → send! room? 2 < 2 false → full → descriptor branch room? false → stays as :probe ✓. outstanding count = 2 ✓.\n\nserve! responds to ids 0 and 1 (mirror reads both requests from :ab — wait, requests went through the forwarding writer to (:ab t)? full-then-forward-writer wraps `(writer (:ab t))` = identity on :ab handle... `capped-reflections` passes `#(full-then-forward-writer % 3)`, and writer is applied to `(:ab t)`. After 3 rejections, forwards to ab. So ab has id 0's request (3rd append? no — 3rd append was rejected). Let me recount appends to the wrapper: 1) a-probe id0 → full. 2) b-probe id1 → full. 3) a-retry id0 → full. 4) b-retry id1 → forwarded. 5) a-retry (inside descriptor rc drain) id0 → forwarded. 6) c's admit attempt → not appended (local reject). So :ab carries id1 then id0. serve! → mirror responds to both (descriptor response for \"a\" id 0 and \"b\" id 1). `(stream/descriptor rc)`'s drain absorbs both → outstanding={} → admit-probe! rc → append id2 (7th → forwarded) → register. `(is (nil? (:probe ...rc)))` ✓. serve! responds to id2. `(stream/descriptor rc)` → drain absorbs → all confirmed, held = 0 ✓.\n\nBoth tests correctly trace the implementation.\n\nAlso check `probes` count in first test: `(op-requests (:ab t) :dao.stream/descriptor)` counts descriptor requests retained on ab ring (capacity 64, all retained). After attach: 1 ✓. After descriptor rb: b's probe appended → 2 ✓. c's is locally rejected (no append) → still 2 ✓. After serve! + descriptor rc → 3 ✓.\n\nHmm — in the first test with cap 1, `(is (= 1 (probes)))` after attach — only a's probe appended; b and c were locally rejected without consuming writer. ✓.\n\nOne point: `confirmed?` uses `(.-state r)` — direct field access to deftype; works in JVM Clojure. Also used by link-state. Fine in cljc tests (existing pattern).\n\nNow overall regression hazard scan:\n\n- `room?`'s dissoc-pending-self logic: no effect on non-probe sends (send-or-count!'s cursor/next, append!, resolve's named send). Their ids aren't in pending. ✓\n- append! at cap: send! rejects → returns full → refl-append returns full without register — matches doc \"append! responds full, effect known\" ✓ (existing behavior from S2b round 1, preserved).\n- resolve at cap: send-named! → send! rejects with full → `(contains? #{ok full} ...)` → retry-read (transport-error retry?) ✓ matches doc line 287.\n- cursor at cap: send-or-count! → send-request! → full, not descriptor → returns r; refl-cursor returns retry-read ✓.\n\n- One hazard: `send-request!`'s full+descriptor retention branch triggers on any descriptor op rejection — including a retried probe where writer responded full but room? is true → re-retain in pending. Already covered.\n\n- What about descriptor probes rejected with outcomes other than full/ok (e.g., closed, transport-error)? cond falls through, returns r. Probe not retained, not registered. Attach already responded ok. Reflection never confirmed, and :probe not set either → admit-probe! does nothing. Reflection's ops: not gone, not closed → cursor/next will send their own requests; descriptor responds locally ok anyway. Confirmation lost until...? Hmm: probe rejected with e.g. writer closed → not retained anywhere. Later ops don't re-probe. Reflection works but unconfirmed (surface nil → surface-lacks? false → all ops attempted). Existing behavior from before S2b? Round 1 code had the same structure (retained only on full). Doc says \"rejected attach probes are retained... only while there is room\" — about full rejections. Rejection via closed writer means channel gone anyway. Not a regression, minor note at most.\n\n- Interaction with drain-budget: retry-pending! runs once per drain regardless of drain-budget ✓ (\"once per drain\").\n\n- `admit-probe!` placed after drain! in all four op paths ✓ (lines 762, 840, 864, 893). And in refl-close: pending cleanup + :probe dissoc before drain — line 924 dissoc :probe then drain! — retry-pending! inside drain could resend... close! first removes pending entries for this refl (lines 918-923), then dissocs :probe → drain's retry-pending! doesn't see them ✓. Good ordering: no probe resurrection after close.\n\n- channel-gone?: admit-probe! guards ✓. retry-pending! inside drain! guarded by drain's own when-not channel-gone ✓.\n\nws-project: `dial`'s select-keys for policy includes max-outstanding/max-filed ✓ (lines 485-486), docstring mentions ✓.\n\nDoc: §2.4 bullets match. Also lines 467-468 mention bounds for dial/attacher propagation.\n\nVerdict: ACCEPT, but note that I couldn't run the test suite in this session (shell blocked) — relying on static trace of both new tests and the implementer's reported run (71 tests / 565 assertions; round 1 baseline was 69/537, delta consistent with two new deftests). Should I accept without running? The task explicitly asks to run tests. Cannot in plan mode. My plan file will be the review report itself, plus a note that execution steps (test run + copy report to collab/) require exiting plan mode.\n\nActually — reconsidering: is there any way to run tests? shell_command is permission-gated and failed. No other execution tool. So no.\n\nAlso let me quickly check whether anything else in the diff might be problematic: per git status, modified files are docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md, src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc, src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc, test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc. Without git diff, can't see what exactly changed against HEAD, but commit e9645a2d was S2a and the S2b work (drain-budget, max-outstanding, max-filed, plus the reconcile) is the uncommitted diff. Reviewed current state of all relevant code paths. ws_project changes: mirror-bounds, step budgets etc — some were committed in S0/S1 (65f635d0 \"step budget, session cap, idle reaping on ws-project\"). Uncommitted ws_project changes are probably the dial policy select-keys for new keys. Verified current file contains them.\n\nAlso check that test file has no other new S2b tests I haven't looked at — sections at line 1815+ covered. counting-writer helper too — existing.\n\nMinor findings (non-blocking) for the report:\n1. `count-ask!`'s resend bypasses `send!` — but resends don't increase retained count so cap invariant holds; if writer is full at that moment, resend is silently dropped and asks resets — existing behavior, note only.\n2. Pending probes survive channel-loss! (not cleared) — bounded by cap, removed on close!/reflection ops; admit-probe! guards on channel-gone? so nothing sent. Cosmetic issue.\n3. Probes rejected with non-full outcomes (e.g., closed) are neither retained nor retried — confirmation deferred indefinitely for that reflection unless re-attached; consistent with doc since channel is effectively gone.\n4. Final assertion message in `a-prefetch-never-evicts-its-own-answer` says \"source's own blocked\" but it's the reflection's pending-send blocked; trivial.\n5. Could not run the suite in this session (shell gated); static trace of both new tests against implementation confirms the logic; counts consistent with implementer's reported 71/565.\n\nEviction order deviation (arrival vs lowest id) is explicit in reconcile report and doc — round 1 review required exactly this (\"any necessary changes to the eviction order stated by the architect should be made explicit\"). ✓ Satisfied.\n\nNow let me write the plan file. Plan file location: ~/.commandcode/plans/. Absolute path needed — ~ expansion: /Users/sto/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2b-review-r2-findings.md. Instructions say use full absolute path starting with ~/. Write to \"/Users/sto/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2b-review-r2-findings.md\"... instructions say \"~/.commandcode/plans/\" — use ~-prefixed path literally? \"ALWAYS use full absolute paths starting with ~/\". OK, use \"~/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2b-review-r2-findings.md\".\n\nPlan will contain: verdict ACCEPT, detailed findings, and execution steps (write findings file to collab/ path, run test command, report results).\n\nWith this, I've now got a complete picture of the whole thing. I statically traced both P1 fixes against the two new regression tests and the documentation — since shell execution is locked down in this session (plan mode + permission gate), I can't run the test suite or do `git diff` here. I'll write up the full review report as a plan file, together with the execution steps for finalizing it.\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_9aadadd679d34a13a7697f39","name":"write_file","input":{"file_path":"~/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2b-review-r2-findings.md","content":"# Plan: Deliver S2b Round-2 review report (verdict: ACCEPT)\n\n## Context\nTask `stream-crossmachine-s2b-review-r2`: independent read-only review of the S2b\nreconciliation on branch `stream-crossmachine-s2` @ `e9645a2d` (worktree\n`/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2`). The review analysis is COMPLETE\n(static trace of code + both new tests + doc). What remains is blocked by plan\nmode: writing the report to `collab/` and running the test suite.\n\n## Verdict: ACCEPT\n\n## Review findings (the report body)\n\n### P1-1 (probe deadlock) — resolved\n`src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc`:\n- New `room?` (L408–418) admits a request only when\n  `outstanding + (pending − self) < max-outstanding`. Both `send!` (L421–428,\n  local refusal as `full`, nothing crossing) and the probe-retention branch of\n  `send-request!` (L451–457) require it, so the invariant\n  `outstanding + pending ≤ max-outstanding` holds on every path.\n- Deadlock elimination traced: a kept probe's retry excludes itself from the\n  count, so with the invariant it always sees ≤ cap−1 and always has room;\n  only the writer itself can refuse it. Round-1's mutual-counting deadlock\n  (two pending probes at cap 1 each refusing the other) is structurally\n  impossible.\n- No-room recovery: the probe waits on its own reflection (`:probe`), offered\n  again by `admit-probe!` (L700–708) right after `drain!` in all four\n  reflection ops (descriptor L762, cursor L840, next L864, append! L893).\n  `refl-close` clears both the reflection's pending entries and `:probe`\n  (L918–924) before draining, so close cannot resurrect a probe.\n  `admit-probe!` guards `closed?` and `channel-gone?`.\n- No double-send interleaving: within one op, `drain!` runs `retry-pending!`\n  before the absorb loop; a successful send clears BOTH `:pending` entry and\n  `:probe` (L446–449), so `admit-probe!` afterwards is a no-op. A probe can\n  transiently sit in both `:pending` and `:probe` (retry refused for no room),\n  but every accepting path clears both markers atomically-enough under the\n  single-driver model. Traced against both test scenarios below.\n\nTest `attach-probes-share-a-capped-link` (test L1992–2031) — hand-traced both\ncases against the implementation:\n- Cap 1, accepting writer: attach a sends (held 1); b, c locally refused onto\n  their reflections (no writer consumption, probes-on-wire stays 1). Each\n  serve!+op admits exactly the next probe. All assertions match the trace;\n  final state: all confirmed, held 0.\n- Cap 2, `full-then-forward-writer 3`: a, b kept in `:pending` (at cap), c on\n  `:probe`. Writer refusals counted: a(full), b(full), a-retry(full),\n  b-retry(ok, outstanding), a-retry(ok, outstanding=2), c locally refused\n  (never appended) — matches assertions `(= 2 (count (:outstanding ...)))`,\n  `(<= (held link) 2)`, `:probe` presence/nil transitions, final held 0.\n\n### P1-2 (prefetch self-eviction) — resolved\n- `stamped-budget` (L384–392): `min(budget, max-filed)`; `wire-request` stamps\n  it on `next`, so the peer is never asked to chase outcomes the link would\n  drop. With max-filed 1 / budget 2 the wire budget is 1 → mirror chase k=1\n  returns no `more` at all (first clamp).\n- `install-more!` (L580–606): installs `min(budget−1, max-filed − filed −\n  filed-cursors)` — prefetch only fills free capacity, never evicts, even\n  against a surplus-sending peer (second, independent clamp).\n- Eviction order changed from lowest-id to arrival order (`:filed-seq` /\n  `next-age!`, L541–547); `file!` (L570–577) files as newest then\n  `evict-filed!` (L550–567) removes min-age until within bound, so filing\n  never evicts the answer just filed. The lowest-id hazard (re-asked answer\n  gets a lower id and evicts itself) is real and correctly identified in the\n  reconcile report; the deviation from the architect's stated ordering is\n  EXPLICIT in both the reconcile report and doc §2.4 L294–299 (\"in the order\n  the link retained them\"), which is what round-1 review demanded.\n- Ordering in `absorb!`: `file!` then `install-more!` (L671–672) — after\n  file!'s eviction the total is ≤ m, and `fits` guarantees install keeps it\n  ≤ m with no second eviction. Loop termination in `evict-filed!` is fine\n  (each pass removes one entry; m ≥ 1 by policy validation).\n\nTest `a-prefetch-never-evicts-its-own-answer` (L2034–2052) — traced: stamped\nwire budget asserted 1; each of next 0/1/2 goes blocked → serve! → returns\n:a/:b/:c; retained ≤ 1 after every step; the mid-loop `blocked` assertion at\nc>0 confirms nothing is prefetched past capacity. Round-1's reproduction\n(perpetual blocked with max-filed 1, budget 2) cannot recur: asked = 0 and\nfits = 0 both independently prevent the install.\n\nTest `more-is-clamped-to-the-links-own-budget` third row updated to\n`[{:budget 4 :max-filed 2} 1 1]` — traced: stamped 2, asked 1, fits\n2−1−0=1 → 1 filed + 1 installed. Correct; the old `0 2` expectation was the\ndefect.\n\n### Doc §2.4 (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md L284–302)\nMatches the implementation exactly: local refusal outcome mappings\n(cursor/resolve → transport-error retry?, next → blocked, append! → full with\nknown effect), room-rule + invariant + probe-waits-on-reflection for\nmax-outstanding; arrival-order eviction, file-never-evicts-itself,\nprefetch-fills-free-capacity, stamped budget ≤ max-filed for max-filed.\n`ws_project.cljc` `dial` propagates max-outstanding/max-filed through the\npolicy select-keys (L481–486) — round-1 contract item confirmed.\n\n### Non-blocking notes (no action required)\n1. `count-ask!` resend (L473) bypasses `send!`; acceptable — a resend does not\n   grow the retained count, so the cap invariant is unaffected (pre-existing).\n2. A probe refused with a non-`full` outcome (e.g. closed writer) is neither\n   kept nor retried; in practice that means the channel is gone, and\n   confirmation is only observable through that reflection anyway.\n3. `:pending` probes survive `channel-loss!` (bounded by cap; cleared by\n   close!; `admit-probe!`/`drain!` guard on `channel-gone?` so nothing sends).\n   Cosmetic.\n4. Final assertion message in the prefetch test says \"the source's own\n   blocked\" though it is the reflection's pending-send blocked. Trivial.\n\n### Verification status\n- Static review: COMPLETE (full read of remote.cljc, ws_project.cljc, both new\n  tests + helpers, doc §2.4, round-1 findings, reconcile report).\n- Test suite: NOT RUN in this session — shell execution is permission-gated\n  (plan mode). Implementer reports 71 tests / 565 assertions / 0 failures;\n  round-1 baseline was 69/537, and the delta is consistent with exactly the\n  two added deftests. Must be re-run as an execution step below before the\n  report is finalized.\n\n## Execution steps (after plan approval / exiting plan mode)\n1. Run in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2`:\n   `clojure -M:test -n dao.stream.remote-test -n dao.stream.ws-project-test`\n   — confirm 71 tests, 565 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors. If the run\n   contradicts the static verdict, amend the report before writing it.\n2. Write the report body above (verdict, findings, notes, and the actual test\n   output replacing the \"NOT RUN\" paragraph) to:\n   `collab/1791357700000-stream-s2b-review-r2.qwen3.8-max.findings.md`\n3. No code/test/doc edits — review is read-only per task contract.\n"}}]}}
{"type":"event","event":{"type":"tool_queued","toolCallId":"call_9aadadd679d34a13a7697f39","toolName":"write_file","input":{"file_path":"~/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2b-review-r2-findings.md","content":"# Plan: Deliver S2b Round-2 review report (verdict: ACCEPT)\n\n## Context\nTask `stream-crossmachine-s2b-review-r2`: independent read-only review of the S2b\nreconciliation on branch `stream-crossmachine-s2` @ `e9645a2d` (worktree\n`/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2`). The review analysis is COMPLETE\n(static trace of code + both new tests + doc). What remains is blocked by plan\nmode: writing the report to `collab/` and running the test suite.\n\n## Verdict: ACCEPT\n\n## Review findings (the report body)\n\n### P1-1 (probe deadlock) — resolved\n`src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc`:\n- New `room?` (L408–418) admits a request only when\n  `outstanding + (pending − self) < max-outstanding`. Both `send!` (L421–428,\n  local refusal as `full`, nothing crossing) and the probe-retention branch of\n  `send-request!` (L451–457) require it, so the invariant\n  `outstanding + pending ≤ max-outstanding` holds on every path.\n- Deadlock elimination traced: a kept probe's retry excludes itself from the\n  count, so with the invariant it always sees ≤ cap−1 and always has room;\n  only the writer itself can refuse it. Round-1's mutual-counting deadlock\n  (two pending probes at cap 1 each refusing the other) is structurally\n  impossible.\n- No-room recovery: the probe waits on its own reflection (`:probe`), offered\n  again by `admit-probe!` (L700–708) right after `drain!` in all four\n  reflection ops (descriptor L762, cursor L840, next L864, append! L893).\n  `refl-close` clears both the reflection's pending entries and `:probe`\n  (L918–924) before draining, so close cannot resurrect a probe.\n  `admit-probe!` guards `closed?` and `channel-gone?`.\n- No double-send interleaving: within one op, `drain!` runs `retry-pending!`\n  before the absorb loop; a successful send clears BOTH `:pending` entry and\n  `:probe` (L446–449), so `admit-probe!` afterwards is a no-op. A probe can\n  transiently sit in both `:pending` and `:probe` (retry refused for no room),\n  but every accepting path clears both markers atomically-enough under the\n  single-driver model. Traced against both test scenarios below.\n\nTest `attach-probes-share-a-capped-link` (test L1992–2031) — hand-traced both\ncases against the implementation:\n- Cap 1, accepting writer: attach a sends (held 1); b, c locally refused onto\n  their reflections (no writer consumption, probes-on-wire stays 1). Each\n  serve!+op admits exactly the next probe. All assertions match the trace;\n  final state: all confirmed, held 0.\n- Cap 2, `full-then-forward-writer 3`: a, b kept in `:pending` (at cap), c on\n  `:probe`. Writer refusals counted: a(full), b(full), a-retry(full),\n  b-retry(ok, outstanding), a-retry(ok, outstanding=2), c locally refused\n  (never appended) — matches assertions `(= 2 (count (:outstanding ...)))`,\n  `(<= (held link) 2)`, `:probe` presence/nil transitions, final held 0.\n\n### P1-2 (prefetch self-eviction) — resolved\n- `stamped-budget` (L384–392): `min(budget, max-filed)`; `wire-request` stamps\n  it on `next`, so the peer is never asked to chase outcomes the link would\n  drop. With max-filed 1 / budget 2 the wire budget is 1 → mirror chase k=1\n  returns no `more` at all (first clamp).\n- `install-more!` (L580–606): installs `min(budget−1, max-filed − filed −\n  filed-cursors)` — prefetch only fills free capacity, never evicts, even\n  against a surplus-sending peer (second, independent clamp).\n- Eviction order changed from lowest-id to arrival order (`:filed-seq` /\n  `next-age!`, L541–547); `file!` (L570–577) files as newest then\n  `evict-filed!` (L550–567) removes min-age until within bound, so filing\n  never evicts the answer just filed. The lowest-id hazard (re-asked answer\n  gets a lower id and evicts itself) is real and correctly identified in the\n  reconcile report; the deviation from the architect's stated ordering is\n  EXPLICIT in both the reconcile report and doc §2.4 L294–299 (\"in the order\n  the link retained them\"), which is what round-1 review demanded.\n- Ordering in `absorb!`: `file!` then `install-more!` (L671–672) — after\n  file!'s eviction the total is ≤ m, and `fits` guarantees install keeps it\n  ≤ m with no second eviction. Loop termination in `evict-filed!` is fine\n  (each pass removes one entry; m ≥ 1 by policy validation).\n\nTest `a-prefetch-never-evicts-its-own-answer` (L2034–2052) — traced: stamped\nwire budget asserted 1; each of next 0/1/2 goes blocked → serve! → returns\n:a/:b/:c; retained ≤ 1 after every step; the mid-loop `blocked` assertion at\nc>0 confirms nothing is prefetched past capacity. Round-1's reproduction\n(perpetual blocked with max-filed 1, budget 2) cannot recur: asked = 0 and\nfits = 0 both independently prevent the install.\n\nTest `more-is-clamped-to-the-links-own-budget` third row updated to\n`[{:budget 4 :max-filed 2} 1 1]` — traced: stamped 2, asked 1, fits\n2−1−0=1 → 1 filed + 1 installed. Correct; the old `0 2` expectation was the\ndefect.\n\n### Doc §2.4 (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md L284–302)\nMatches the implementation exactly: local refusal outcome mappings\n(cursor/resolve → transport-error retry?, next → blocked, append! → full with\nknown effect), room-rule + invariant + probe-waits-on-reflection for\nmax-outstanding; arrival-order eviction, file-never-evicts-itself,\nprefetch-fills-free-capacity, stamped budget ≤ max-filed for max-filed.\n`ws_project.cljc` `dial` propagates max-outstanding/max-filed through the\npolicy select-keys (L481–486) — round-1 contract item confirmed.\n\n### Non-blocking notes (no action required)\n1. `count-ask!` resend (L473) bypasses `send!`; acceptable — a resend does not\n   grow the retained count, so the cap invariant is unaffected (pre-existing).\n2. A probe refused with a non-`full` outcome (e.g. closed writer) is neither\n   kept nor retried; in practice that means the channel is gone, and\n   confirmation is only observable through that reflection anyway.\n3. `:pending` probes survive `channel-loss!` (bounded by cap; cleared by\n   close!; `admit-probe!`/`drain!` guard on `channel-gone?` so nothing sends).\n   Cosmetic.\n4. Final assertion message in the prefetch test says \"the source's own\n   blocked\" though it is the reflection's pending-send blocked. Trivial.\n\n### Verification status\n- Static review: COMPLETE (full read of remote.cljc, ws_project.cljc, both new\n  tests + helpers, doc §2.4, round-1 findings, reconcile report).\n- Test suite: NOT RUN in this session — shell execution is permission-gated\n  (plan mode). Implementer reports 71 tests / 565 assertions / 0 failures;\n  round-1 baseline was 69/537, and the delta is consistent with exactly the\n  two added deftests. Must be re-run as an execution step below before the\n  report is finalized.\n\n## Execution steps (after plan approval / exiting plan mode)\n1. Run in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2`:\n   `clojure -M:test -n dao.stream.remote-test -n dao.stream.ws-project-test`\n   — confirm 71 tests, 565 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors. If the run\n   contradicts the static verdict, amend the report before writing it.\n2. Write the report body above (verdict, findings, notes, and the actual test\n   output replacing the \"NOT RUN\" paragraph) to:\n   `collab/1791357700000-stream-s2b-review-r2.qwen3.8-max.findings.md`\n3. No code/test/doc edits — review is read-only per task contract.\n"}}}
