escriptor ra)`: drain → retry-pending!: send id 0: room? 0 + {1} =1 <2 → append → full (3rd time) → room? → re-retain in pending. Send id 1: room? 0 + {0} = 1 < 2 → append → forwards (n exhausted) → ok → register outstanding={1}, pending={0}. Then loop reads: nothing on :ba. admit-probe! ra: no :probe on ra. held = 1+1 = 2 ≤ 2 ✓.\n\n`(stream/descriptor rc)`: drain → retry-pending!: id 0: room? outstanding {1} =1 + pending dissoc 0 = 0 → 1 < 2 → append → ok → register outstanding={0,1}, pending={}. Read: nothing. admit-probe! rc: :probe → send-request! → send! room? 2 < 2 false → full → descriptor branch room? false → stays as :probe ✓. outstanding count = 2 ✓.\n\nserve! responds to ids 0 and 1 (mirror reads both requests from :ab — wait, requests went through the forwarding writer to (:ab t)? full-then-forward-writer wraps `(writer (:ab t))` = identity on :ab handle... `capped-reflections` passes `#(full-then-forward-writer % 3)`, and writer is applied to `(:ab t)`. After 3 rejections, forwards to ab. So ab has id 0's request (3rd append? no — 3rd append was rejected). Let me recount appends to the wrapper: 1) a-probe id0 → full. 2) b-probe id1 → full. 3) a-retry id0 → full. 4) b-retry id1 → forwarded. 5) a-retry (inside descriptor rc drain) id0 → forwarded. 6) c's admit attempt → not appended (local reject). So :ab carries id1 then id0. serve! → mirror responds to both (descriptor response for \"a\" id 0 and \"b\" id 1). `(stream/descriptor rc)`'s drain absorbs both → outstanding={} → admit-probe! rc → append id2 (7th → forwarded) → register. `(is (nil? (:probe ...rc)))` ✓. serve! responds to id2. `(stream/descriptor rc)` → drain absorbs → all confirmed, held = 0 ✓.\n\nBoth tests correctly trace the implementation.\n\nAlso check `probes` count in first test: `(op-requests (:ab t) :dao.stream/descriptor)` counts descriptor requests retained on ab ring (capacity 64, all retained). After attach: 1 ✓. After descriptor rb: b's probe appended → 2 ✓. c's is locally rejected (no append) → still 2 ✓. After serve! + descriptor rc → 3 ✓.\n\nHmm — in the first test with cap 1, `(is (= 1 (probes)))` after attach — only a's probe appended; b and c were locally rejected without consuming writer. ✓.\n\nOne point: `confirmed?` uses `(.-state r)` — direct field access to deftype; works in JVM Clojure. Also used by link-state. Fine in cljc tests (existing pattern).\n\nNow overall regression hazard scan:\n\n- `room?`'s dissoc-pending-self logic: no effect on non-probe sends (send-or-count!'s cursor/next, append!, resolve's named send). Their ids aren't in pending. ✓\n- append! at cap: send! rejects → returns full → refl-append returns full without register — matches doc \"append! responds full, effect known\" ✓ (existing behavior from S2b round 1, preserved).\n- resolve at cap: send-named! → send! rejects with full → `(contains? #{ok full} ...)` → retry-read (transport-error retry?) ✓ matches doc line 287.\n- cursor at cap: send-or-count! → send-request! → full, not descriptor → returns r; refl-cursor returns retry-read ✓.\n\n- One hazard: `send-request!`'s full+descriptor retention branch triggers on any descriptor op rejection — including a retried probe where writer responded full but room? is true → re-retain in pending. Already covered.\n\n- What about descriptor probes rejected with outcomes other than full/ok (e.g., closed, transport-error)? cond falls through, returns r. Probe not retained, not registered. Attach already responded ok. Reflection never confirmed, and :probe not set either → admit-probe! does nothing. Reflection's ops: not gone, not closed → cursor/next will send their own requests; descriptor responds locally ok anyway. Confirmation lost until...? Hmm: probe rejected with e.g. writer closed → not retained anywhere. Later ops don't re-probe. Reflection works but unconfirmed (surface nil → surface-lacks? false → all ops attempted). Existing behavior from before S2b? Round 1 code had the same structure (retained only on full